Section: Lookup

 By codepoint, in emoji_frames.zig's own switch order. An unknown codepoint
 draws nothing, which is what `orelse return` does in the zig.

  emoji-polys-for : Integer -> List StillPoly
  emoji-polys-for (cp) =
    if cp == 129414 then duck-polys
    else if cp == 128024 then elephant-polys
    else if cp == 129426 then giraffe-polys
    else if cp == 129427 then zebra-polys
    else if cp == 129423 then rhino-polys
    else if cp == 128002 then bull-polys
    else if cp == 128004 then cow-polys
    else if cp == 128022 then pig-polys
    else []

Chapter: Critter
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Stills
  cites Safari chapter EmojiStills

 A roadside animal as a baked-polygon billboard.

 A port of wasm/critter.zig. It projects the feet and the top to get a pixel
 height, then transforms the baked unit-frame polygons to that screen anchor and
 emits them. It knows how to PLACE and FLIP a billboard and nothing else -- where
 the animals stand is world.zig and safari_critter.zig, both already ported, and
 what they look like is the baked table `Safari chapter EmojiStills`.

 THE HEIGHT IS MEASURED IN PIXELS, NOT METRES, and that is the whole trick of a
 billboard. Project the feet, project a point `height` metres above them, and the
 gap between the two screen y values is how tall the animal is on this frame at
 this distance. Everything in the unit frame then scales by that one number, so
 perspective is applied ONCE, to a scalar, rather than per vertex.

 UNDER A PIXEL IT DRAWS NOTHING. The zig returns early, and so does this: a
 sub-pixel animal is not worth a polygon, and the collector's own size cull has
 usually caught it long before.

 THE BULL IS SHADED NOW, and it was the last animal that was not. Seven of the
 eight are the Fluent Flat art and solid throughout; the bull is the Color variant
 and 40 of its 43 polygons carry a 2-stop gradient, which the baker used to
 flatten to its first stop because Paint modelled tags 0, 1 and 3 only. Paint has
 5 and 6, the table carries the stops and the geometry, and the arm below emits
 them -- so the bull grades like every other animal instead of being excluded.

 A POINT AND A VECTOR MAP DIFFERENTLY, and that is the one thing in this chapter
 easy to get wrong. A gradient's linear endpoints and its radial CENTRE are
 points: they translate to the feet anchor. Its radial AXES are vectors: they only
 scale and flip. Mapping an axis as a point would anchor the ellipse's shape to
 the animal's feet, which looks like a shading bug and is really a coordinate one.

 We say:

Section: The Billboard Map

 A POINT translates to the feet anchor and scales by the pixel height; the art is
 baked facing LEFT, so an animal that faces right mirrors in x. Mirrors mapP.

  map-p : ScreenPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> ScreenPt
  map-p (b) (s) (ht) (x) (y) =
    ScreenPt { x = b.x + s * x * ht, y = b.y - y * ht }

  facing : Boolean -> Real
  facing (face-right) = if face-right then 0.0 - 1.0 else 1.0

  mapped-pts : ScreenPt, Real, Real, List StillPt, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  mapped-pts (b) (s) (ht) (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [map-p b s ht (list-at ps i).x (list-at ps i).y] & mapped-pts b s ht ps (i + 1)

 The zig's screen buffer holds 512 points and a longer polygon is SKIPPED rather
 than truncated -- the same choice emit-ground makes about its eight, and for the
 same reason: a silently shortened polygon is a wrong shape drawn confidently.
 Nothing in the baked table comes close, but the guard is the zig's and is kept.

  max-critter-pts : Integer
  max-critter-pts = 512

 A VECTOR takes the mirror and the scale and NOT the translation. Mirrors mapV.

  map-v : Real, Real, Real, Real -> ScreenPt
  map-v (s) (ht) (x) (y) =
    ScreenPt { x = s * x * ht, y = 0.0 - y * ht }

  critter-poly : ScreenPt, Real, Real, StillPoly -> List DrawCmd
  critter-poly (b) (s) (ht) (poly) =
    if list-length poly.pts > max-critter-pts then []
    else if list-length poly.grad > 0 then critter-grad b s ht poly (list-at poly.grad 0)
    else push-poly poly.color (mapped-pts b s ht poly.pts 0)

  critter-grad : ScreenPt, Real, Real, StillPoly, StillGrad -> List DrawCmd
  critter-grad (b) (s) (ht) (poly) (g) =
    let pts = mapped-pts b s ht poly.pts 0
    in if g.kind == 1 then critter-linear b s ht g pts else critter-radial b s ht g pts

  critter-linear : ScreenPt, Real, Real, StillGrad, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  critter-linear (b) (s) (ht) (g) (pts) =
    let p0 = map-p b s ht g.ax g.ay
    in let p1 = map-p b s ht g.bx g.by
    in push-linear-grad-poly g.rgba0 g.rgba1 g.off0 g.off1 p0.x p0.y p1.x p1.y pts

  critter-radial : ScreenPt, Real, Real, StillGrad, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  critter-radial (b) (s) (ht) (g) (pts) =
    let c = map-p b s ht g.cx g.cy
    in let u = map-v s ht g.ux g.uy
    in let v = map-v s ht g.vx g.vy
    in push-radial-grad-poly g.rgba0 g.rgba1 g.off0 g.off1 c.x c.y u.x u.y v.x v.y pts

  critter-polys : ScreenPt, Real, Real, List StillPoly, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  critter-polys (b) (s) (ht) (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else (critter-poly b s ht (list-at ps i)) & critter-polys b s ht ps (i + 1)

Section: Drawing One

 Painted in array order, which is the SVG document order the baker preserved --
 painter's order, so a later polygon covers an earlier one.

  critter-draw : Real, Real, Real, Integer, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  critter-draw (right) (forward) (height) (cp) (face-right) (cf) (view-w) =
    let base = project (Vec3 { right = right, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let top = project (Vec3 { right = right, forward = forward, height = height }) cf view-w
    in let h = base.y - top.y
    in if h < 1.0 then []
      else critter-polys base (facing face-right) h (emoji-polys-for cp) 0

Chapter: CatStills
  cites Safari chapter Stills

 GENERATED by harness/bake_stills.py from wasm/cat_frames.zig.
 Do not edit: re-run the script instead.

 The crossing cat as a FLIPBOOK of seven named stills -- rest, stride, frozen,
 coil, flight, land, collapse -- in the unit frame the billboards use: feet at
 y = 0, y up, standing height 1, x toward the tail, facing LEFT.

 SEVEN STILLS, NOT SEVEN HUNDRED. Safari chapter Cat's `cat-state` picks which
 one per crossing step and computes the lateral travel and the hop continuously,
 so the animation is COMPUTED and only the shapes are looked up. cat_anatomy's
 fills are baked as polygons and its strokes as thin quads; all are solid, so
 unlike the animals nothing here is flattened.

 We say:

