, grad = [], pts = [StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.6771), y = 0.3385 }, StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.4771), y = 0.329 }, StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.4765), y = 0.341 }, StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.6765), y = 0.3505 }] }, StillPoly { color = 3811863, grad = [], pts = [StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.6764), y = 0.349 }, StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.4764), y = 0.364 }, StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.4772), y = 0.376 }, StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.6772), y = 0.361 }] }, StillPoly { color = 3811863, grad = [], pts = [StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.6756), y = 0.3596 }, StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.4756), y = 0.3991 }, StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.478), y = 0.4109 }, StillPt { x = (0.0 - 0.678), y = 0.3714 }] }]

Section: Lookup

 By pose index, which is what cat-state returns. An index outside the
 flipbook draws nothing.

  cat-polys-for : Integer -> List StillPoly
  cat-polys-for (i) =
    if i == 0 then pose-rest-polys
    else if i == 1 then pose-stride-polys
    else if i == 2 then pose-frozen-polys
    else if i == 3 then pose-coil-polys
    else if i == 4 then pose-flight-polys
    else if i == 5 then pose-land-polys
    else if i == 6 then pose-collapse-polys
    else []

Chapter: CatDraw
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Stills
  cites Safari chapter CatStills

 The crossing cat's flipbook still, drawn as a billboard.

 A port of wasm/cat.zig's `draw`, and the rest of that file is `Safari chapter
 Cat`. SPLIT FOR A NAMESPACE-WEIGHT REASON, not a logical one: world.zig places a
 cat on every segment, so World cites Cat, so EVERY check that touches the world
 would drag 534 KB of baked stills into its bundle for the sake of a five-constant
 placement record. Keeping the art on this side means only the chapters that
 actually draw a cat pay for it. Pose was split out of Rider for a comparable
 reason -- there to cut a cite cycle, here to cut a cite's weight.

 THE HOP IS APPLIED IN THE UNIT FRAME, NOT TO THE ANCHOR, and it is the one thing
 worth reading twice. The feet are projected at height ZERO and the lift is added
 to each polygon's own y before scaling -- `base.y - (p.y + lift) * h` -- so the
 hop is measured in CAT-HEIGHTS and scales with distance like the rest of the
 still. Projecting the anchor at the lift instead would be a metres-based hop that
 grows wrong as the cat recedes. (The spike's throwaway marker did exactly that,
 which is a fair illustration of the difference being small and real.)

 NO HORIZONTAL FLIP. The art faces LEFT, which is the escape direction, and the
 cat only ever crosses one way -- so unlike the animals there is no facing to pass
 in and no mirror to apply.

 NO NEAR-CLIP EITHER. It is a billboard at a single depth, and render only
 collects it when it is already in front of the near plane, so there is nothing to
 clip against. Ground quads clip because they are big and flat and can straddle
 the eye; this cannot.

 We say:

Section: Drawing One Still

 The zig's screen buffer holds 256 points here -- half what critter.zig gives an
 animal -- and a longer polygon is SKIPPED rather than truncated. The largest
 baked cat polygon is 216 points, so the guard never fires on this art, but a
 silently shortened polygon would be a wrong shape drawn confidently and the zig
 declines to draw one.

  max-cat-pts : Integer
  max-cat-pts = 256

  cat-pt : ScreenPt, Real, Real, StillPt -> ScreenPt
  cat-pt (b) (h) (lift) (p) =
    ScreenPt { x = b.x + p.x * h, y = b.y - (p.y + lift) * h }

  cat-pts : ScreenPt, Real, Real, List StillPt, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  cat-pts (b) (h) (lift) (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [cat-pt b h lift (list-at ps i)] & cat-pts b h lift ps (i + 1)

  cat-poly : ScreenPt, Real, Real, StillPoly -> List DrawCmd
  cat-poly (b) (h) (lift) (poly) =
    if list-length poly.pts > max-cat-pts then []
    else push-poly poly.color (cat-pts b h lift poly.pts 0)

  cat-polys : ScreenPt, Real, Real, List StillPoly, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  cat-polys (b) (h) (lift) (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else (cat-poly b h lift (list-at ps i)) & cat-polys b h lift ps (i + 1)

Section: The Billboard

 The pixel height is the gap between the projected feet and a point `height` metres
 above them, exactly as an animal's is. Under a pixel it draws nothing; the
 renderer's size cull is the real cutoff and has usually caught it first.

  cat-draw : Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  cat-draw (right) (forward) (height) (pose-idx) (lift) (cf) (view-w) =
    let base = project (Vec3 { right = right, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let top = project (Vec3 { right = right, forward = forward, height = height }) cf view-w
    in let h = base.y - top.y
    in if h < 1.0 then []
      else cat-polys base h lift (cat-polys-for pose-idx) 0

Chapter: Pond

 pond -- a small pond at a corner (seg13), just BEYOND a right-turn intersection,
 off to its LEFT, with ducks floating on the water. Pure data and dimensions; the
 mapping, projection and emit live in the renderer, the same split safari-critter
 uses.

 Frame: cv = metres BEYOND the incoming segment's end edge (the rider's forward as
 he arrives), cu = metres across from its end-left corner (0 = left edge, + = toward
 the road; the pond's cu is NEGATIVE, out to the left -- the outer side of the right
 turn).

 A port of wasm/pond.zig. The zig carries these as f32 and this chapter as Real
 (f64), so the judge compares with a tolerance rather than for equality; every
 value here is integral except duck-height, so the tolerance buys nothing yet and
 the comparison is effectively exact.

 We say:

Section: Shapes

  PondPt = record {
    cu : Real,
    cv : Real
  }

  Duck = record {
    p : PondPt,
    face-right : Boolean
  }

Section: Water

 The outline is a big blob off the LEFT of the road, ~30m across (cu -1 to -31)
 reaching ~30m beyond the intersection (cv 3 to 32). Its near edge (the flat front
 at cv 3) faces the incoming road.

  water-outline : List PondPt
  water-outline =
    [ PondPt { cu = -2.0, cv = 3.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -28.0, cv = 3.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -31.0, cv = 14.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -26.0, cv = 28.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -15.0, cv = 32.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -5.0, cv = 29.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -1.0, cv = 16.0 } ]

  water-color : Integer
  water-color = 3112588

Section: Bank

 A 1m khaki bank along the water's FAR edge -- the shore. Its inner edge hugs the
 water's far edge (the arc cv 29 to 32 to 28), its outer edge is 1m further onto
 the land.

  bank : List PondPt
  bank =
    [ PondPt { cu = -5.0, cv = 29.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -15.0, cv = 32.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -26.0, cv = 28.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -26.0, cv = 29.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -15.0, cv = 33.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -5.0, cv = 30.0 } ]

  bank-color : Integer
  bank-color = 12759680

Section: Ducks

 Six ducks floating on the water, spread across the pond -- three deeper in (larger
 cv) and three nearer the intersection (smaller cv, toward the front edge at cv 3).
 ADULTS, drawn slightly out of proportion so they read. cu is shifted toward the
 road (less negative) than the pond centre, so the ducks hug the near/road side of
 the water while still sitting inside the outline.

  duck-codepoint : Integer
  duck-codepoint = 129414

  duck-height : Real
  duck-height = 0.9

  ducks : List Duck
  ducks =
    [ Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -8.0, cv = 11.0 }, face-right = True },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -16.0, cv = 17.0 }, face-right = False },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -9.0, cv = 21.0 }, face-right = True },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -4.0, cv = 6.0 }, face-right = True },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -12.0, cv = 7.0 }, face-right = False },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -20.0, cv = 8.0 }, face-right = True } ]

Chapter: Tower
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The radio tower beyond each intersection: a tall square-base lattice pyramid of
 metal rods tapering to an apex with a pink beacon. The owner places it; this
 chapter only renders.

 A port of wasm/tower.zig.

 THIS IS THE FLAT FORM ONLY -- the four faces drawn as a billboard lattice, which
 is what the TypeScript draws beyond its far distance. The zig diverges from the
 TypeScript in ONE way and the port keeps the divergence: every rod stays a 3D
 segment and is near-clipped BEFORE projecting, rather than interpolating points
 that are already on screen. The TypeScript could skip that because it only used
 the flat path for far towers, where nothing straddles the eye plane; here the
 flat path runs at all distances, so a tower you pass close would otherwise streak
 a rod clear across the viewport.

 The apex beacon BLINKS on the step clock, as a pure function of it, so it freezes
 on pause and runs backwards on reverse -- which is why the phase can be negative
 and why the modulo has to be the floored one.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

  tower-height : Real
  tower-height = 80.0

 Half the twelve-metre square base edge.

  tower-half : Real
  tower-half = 6.0

 A cross-beam ring every this many metres, and X-braces on the bottom two stages.

  stage-height : Real
  stage-height = 20.0

  brace-stages : Integer
  brace-stages = 2

  rod-half : Real
  rod-half = 0.12

  rod-w : Real
  rod-w = rod-half * 2.0

  tower-metal : Integer
  tower-metal = 10133672

 A local ground bulge, stronger than the road's, so a far tower sinks until its
 base meets the horizon.

  earth-radius : Real
  earth-radius = 20000.0

  beacon-radius : Real
  beacon-radius = 3.0

  beacon-color : Integer
  beacon-color = 16723942

 Frames for a full invisible-to-bright-to-invisible blink.

  beacon-period : Real
  beacon-period = 120.0

Section: The Beacon Clock

 Spread each tower's blink phase so they do not pulse in unison: 37 is coprime to
 the 120-frame period, so successive owners land on distinct offsets.

  beacon-offset-for : Integer -> Real
  beacon-offset-for (n) = real-from-int ((n * 37) - (((n * 37) / 120) * 120))

 Brightness is 0 at the cycle ends and 1 in the middle.

 THE DOUBLE MODULO IS THE ZIG'S AND IS KEPT. `@mod` is already non-negative for a
 positive divisor, so `@mod(@mod(p, P) + P, P)` is belt and braces -- but the port
 is a port, and a phase really can be negative here because the clock runs
 backwards on reverse.

  beacon-brightness : Real -> Real
  beacon-brightness (phase) =
    let wrapped = mod-real (mod-real phase beacon-period + beacon-period) beacon-period
    in (1.0 - r-cos (2.0 * pi * wrapped / beacon-period)) / 2.0

Section: The Square

 The four base corners in half-base units, before the yaw.

  corner-u : Integer -> Real
  corner-u (k) = if k == 0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else if k == 1 then 1.0 else if k == 2 then 1.0 else 0.0 - 1.0

  corner-v : Integer -> Real
  corner-v (k) = if k == 0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else if k == 1 then 0.0 - 1.0 else if k == 2 then 1.0 else 1.0

 Base corner k in the owning segment's bottom-left frame, given the tower's base
 centre and yaw. The caller maps it into the rider frame. At the apex the square
 shrinks to a point at the centre, so the apex needs no helper.

  base-corner-ax : Integer, Real, Real, Real -> AX
  base-corner-ax (k) (a0) (x0) (yaw) =
    let du = corner-u k * tower-half
    in let dv = corner-v k * tower-half
    in let cy = r-cos yaw
    in let sy = r-sin yaw
    in AX { a = a0 + (du * sy + dv * cy), x = x0 + (du * cy - dv * sy) }

Section: Geometry

  tower-ground-drop : RiderPt -> Real
  tower-ground-drop (p) = (p.right * p.right + p.forward * p.forward) / (2.0 * earth-radius)

  lerp3v : Vec3, Vec3, Real -> Vec3
  lerp3v (a) (b) (t) =
    Vec3 {
      right = a.right + (b.right - a.right) * t,
      forward = a.forward + (b.forward - a.forward) * t,
      height = a.height + (b.height - a.height) * t
    }

 Cross-section corner k at world height h, lowered by the ground drop. The square
 shrinks linearly toward the apex at the centre, so it is a base-to-centre lerp by
 h over the height. The corner stays a 3D point so each rod can be near-clipped
 before projecting.

  corner-at : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Integer, Real, Real -> Vec3
  corner-at (base) (center) (k) (h) (drop) =
    let t = h / tower-height
    in let bk = list-at base k
    in Vec3 { right = bk.right + (center.right - bk.right) * t, forward = bk.forward + (center.forward - bk.forward) * t, height = h - drop }

Section: Rods

 A thick screen-space line as a filled quad of width wpx.

  bar : ScreenPt, ScreenPt, Real -> List DrawCmd
  bar (a) (b) (wpx) =
    let dx = b.x - a.x
    in let dy = b.y - a.y
    in let raw = real-sqrt (dx * dx + dy * dy)
    in let len = if raw < 0.0001 then 1.0 else raw
    in rod-quad a b ((0.0 - dy) / len * wpx / 2.0) (dx / len * wpx / 2.0)

 NAMED FOR ITS SECTION RATHER THAN ITS SHAPE, and the rename was forced by the
 bare-metal arm: `bar-quad` is also GuardRail's, and Codex chapters share one flat
 namespace. Each chapter sees its own definition so both compiled and ran
 correctly -- but a mention in a chapter that defines NEITHER resolves by the
 order the build globs files, which is not a property anything should rely on.
 The seed diagnoses it (CDX3006); the zig arm says nothing.

  rod-quad : ScreenPt, ScreenPt, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  rod-quad (a) (b) (ox) (oy) =
    push-poly tower-metal [ScreenPt { x = a.x + ox, y = a.y + oy }, ScreenPt { x = b.x + ox, y = b.y + oy }, ScreenPt { x = b.x - ox, y = b.y - oy }, ScreenPt { x = a.x - ox, y = a.y - oy }]

 One rod, near-clipped: a 3D segment clipped to the near plane before projecting,
 so a rod straddling the eye plane cannot fling a quad across the viewport. Both
 ends behind means skip; one behind means pull it to the plane.

  bar3d : Vec3, Vec3, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  bar3d (a) (b) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) =
    let a-in = a.forward >= near
    in let b-in = b.forward >= near
    in if a-in then (if b-in then bar3d-draw a b wpx cf view-w else bar3d-draw a (bar3d-cut a b) wpx cf view-w)
       else (if b-in then bar3d-draw (bar3d-cut a b) b wpx cf view-w else [])

  bar3d-cut : Vec3, Vec3 -> Vec3
  bar3d-cut (a) (b) = lerp3v a b ((near - a.forward) / (b.forward - a.forward))

  bar3d-draw : Vec3, Vec3, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  bar3d-draw (a) (b) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) =
    bar (project a cf view-w) (project b cf view-w) wpx

Section: The Tower

 Rod width in pixels at this depth, from two ground points one metre apart.

  rod-px : Real, Real, Real -> Real
  rod-px (forward) (cf) (view-w) =
    let p1 = project (Vec3 { right = 1.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let p0 = project (Vec3 { right = 0.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in rod-w * (p1.x - p0.x)


  tower-legs : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Vec3, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  tower-legs (base) (center) (apex) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else (bar3d (corner-at base center k clip-h drop) apex wpx cf view-w) & tower-legs base center apex clip-h drop wpx cf view-w (k + 1)

 Cross-beam rings at 20, 40 and 60, above the drop.

  ring-at : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  ring-at (base) (center) (h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else (bar3d (corner-at base center k h drop) (corner-at base center ((k + 1) - (((k + 1) / 4) * 4)) h drop) wpx cf view-w) & ring-at base center h drop wpx cf view-w (k + 1)

  rings : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  rings (base) (center) (h) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) =
    if h >= tower-height then []
    else (if h <= clip-h then [] else ring-at base center h drop wpx cf view-w 0) & rings base center (h + stage-height) clip-h drop wpx cf view-w

 X-braces on the bottom stages, each diagonal clipped at the drop height.

  brace-at : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  brace-at (base) (center) (lo) (hi) (f) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else brace-pair base center lo hi f drop wpx cf view-w k

  brace-pair : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  brace-pair (base) (center) (lo) (hi) (f) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    let j = (k + 1) - (((k + 1) / 4) * 4)
    in let kj = bar3d (lerp3v (corner-at base center k lo drop) (corner-at base center j hi drop) f) (corner-at base center j hi drop) wpx cf view-w
    in let jk = bar3d (lerp3v (corner-at base center j lo drop) (corner-at base center k hi drop) f) (corner-at base center k hi drop) wpx cf view-w
    in kj & jk & brace-at base center lo hi f drop wpx cf view-w (k + 1)

  braces : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  braces (base) (center) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (stage) =
    if stage >= brace-stages then []
    else brace-stage base center clip-h drop wpx cf view-w stage

  brace-stage : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  brace-stage (base) (center) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (stage) =
    let lo = real-from-int stage * stage-height
    in let hi = lo + stage-height
    in let rest = braces base center clip-h drop wpx cf view-w (stage + 1)
    in if hi <= clip-h then rest
       else (brace-at base center lo hi ((real-max lo clip-h - lo) / stage-height) drop wpx cf view-w 0) & rest

 The apex beacon: a pink disc whose SIZE scales with distance but whose BRIGHTNESS
 is the blink alpha -- a depthless glow composited over whatever is behind. Skips
 the invisible part of the cycle, and a sub-pixel disc.

  draw-beacon : ScreenPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-beacon (apex-s) (forward) (cf) (view-w) (bright) =
    if bright < 0.02 then []
    else beacon-disc apex-s forward cf view-w bright

  beacon-disc : ScreenPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  beacon-disc (apex-s) (forward) (cf) (view-w) (bright) =
    let p1 = project (Vec3 { right = 1.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let p0 = project (Vec3 { right = 0.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let r = beacon-radius * (p1.x - p0.x)
    in if r < 0.5 then [] else push-beacon beacon-color apex-s.x apex-s.y r bright

 Render a tower given its four base corners and base centre already mapped into
 the rider frame. Sinks it by the local ground drop; a tower sunk past its own
 height is below the horizon and is not drawn at all.

  draw-flat : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-flat (base) (center) (cf) (view-w) (beacon-phase) =
    if center.forward < near then []
    else draw-flat-body base center cf view-w beacon-phase (tower-ground-drop center)

  draw-flat-body : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-flat-body (base) (center) (cf) (view-w) (beacon-phase) (drop) =
    if drop >= tower-height then []
    else draw-flat-rods base center cf view-w beacon-phase drop

  draw-flat-rods : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-flat-rods (base) (center) (cf) (view-w) (beacon-phase) (drop) =
    let apex = Vec3 { right = center.right, forward = center.forward, height = tower-height - drop }
    in let wpx = rod-px center.forward cf view-w
    in let l = tower-legs base center apex drop drop wpx cf view-w 0
    in let r = rings base center stage-height drop drop wpx cf view-w
    in let x = braces base center drop drop wpx cf view-w 0
    in let b = draw-beacon (project apex cf view-w) center.forward cf view-w (beacon-brightness beacon-phase)
    in l & r & x & b

Chapter: GuardRail
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The metal barrier on the outer edge of a corner: one horizontal bar on thin
 upright posts. Render builds the PATH the rail follows along the ground -- run-up
 along the outer shoulder, the two legs into and out of the apex, run-out -- and
 this chapter raises that path into 3D rail polygons.

 A port of wasm/guard_rail.zig.

 WHY IT COLLECTS INSTEAD OF DRAWING. Rails, trees and critters share ONE
 back-to-front depth pass, so a tree standing behind a rail is occluded by it.
 Each rail poly therefore carries its own mean forward depth and is merged into
 render's sort; the clip-project-push happens later, in sorted order. Drawing
 rails in a separate pass under all scenery is the exact bug that once made a far
 tree paint over a near rail. So rail-emit and rail-draw-poly are deliberately two
 functions, and the ORDERING BETWEEN THEM BELONGS TO RENDER.

 The bar is a quad STRIP -- one quad per path segment -- not the single long
 ribbon the TypeScript draws. Perceptually identical, since consecutive quads
 share an exact edge, but every poly stays at four vertices, so it near-clips
 cleanly and sorts by an honest per-poly depth. Heights are raw, with no ground
 curvature, exactly as in the TypeScript: the rail is always close and the drop is
 sub-millimetre.

 THIS IS THE FIRST CHAPTER TO CITE `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` RATHER THAN THE
 PORT'S OWN STAND-IN. real-sqrt does not call dm-reduce, so it never reaches the
 real-from-int the zig plug cannot emit, and the emitter emits only what the entry
 reaches. `Safari chapter Trig` is still needed for sine and cosine and is still a
 stand-in; the square root is not one. Nothing here should acquire a Trig name.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

 The bar's centre above the ground, its vertical thickness, and each upright
 post's width -- all in metres.

  rail-height : Real
  rail-height = 0.5

  rail-thickness : Real
  rail-thickness = 0.1

  rail-post-width : Real
  rail-post-width = 0.02

 The bar reads bright metallic; the posts sit a touch darker behind it.

  rail-metal : Integer
  rail-metal = 12765135

  rail-post-metal : Integer
  rail-post-metal = 10133672

 How far the rail runs past the corner along each outer edge. Render's to use, but
 it is the rail's dimension and the zig publishes it here.

  rail-runout : Integer
  rail-runout = 10

 The zig's store is a fixed array whose add silently returns once full. The cap is
 sized so that cannot fire on this route, but a silent drop is still the stated
 behaviour, so the port keeps it -- as a list-take over the finished list, which is
 exactly the same thing: bars are added before posts in both, so truncating the
 concatenation drops precisely the elements the zig would never have stored.

  max-rail-polys : Integer
  max-rail-polys = 3072

  bar-top : Real
  bar-top = rail-height + rail-thickness / 2.0

  bar-bot : Real
  bar-bot = rail-height - rail-thickness / 2.0

  half-post : Real
  half-post = rail-post-width / 2.0

Section: A Raised Poly

 One raised rail poly in the rider frame -- always four vertices -- carrying its
 fill colour and its mean forward depth, the key render sorts it by.

  RailPoly = record {
    v : List Vec3,
    color : Integer,
    fwd : Real
  }

  rail-poly : Vec3, Vec3, Vec3, Vec3, Integer -> RailPoly
  rail-poly (p0) (p1) (p2) (p3) (color) =
    let fwd = (p0.forward + p1.forward + p2.forward + p3.forward) / 4.0
    in RailPoly { v = [p0, p1, p2, p3], color = color, fwd = fwd }

Section: The Bar

 A quad strip between consecutive path points, bottom edge at bar-bot and top edge
 at bar-top. Consecutive quads share an edge exactly, so it reads as one band.

  bar-quad : RiderPt, RiderPt -> RailPoly
  bar-quad (p) (q) =
    let p-bot = Vec3 { right = p.right, forward = p.forward, height = bar-bot }
    in let q-bot = Vec3 { right = q.right, forward = q.forward, height = bar-bot }
    in let q-top = Vec3 { right = q.right, forward = q.forward, height = bar-top }
    in let p-top = Vec3 { right = p.right, forward = p.forward, height = bar-top }
    in rail-poly p-bot q-bot q-top p-top rail-metal

  bars : List RiderPt, Integer -> List RailPoly
  bars (path) (i) =
    if i + 1 >= list-length path then []
    else [bar-quad (list-at path i) (list-at path (i + 1))] & bars path (i + 1)

Section: The Posts

 A slim upright at each path point, its width laid ALONG the local run direction so
 it reads edge-on from the road. The direction is taken from the neighbours on
 either side, clamped at both ends of the path.

 THE ZERO TEST IS ~0, BITWISE EXACT, NOT ~. The zig writes `if (len == 0)`, which
 is a bitwise comparison, and Codex refuses a bare == between Reals (CDX2085), so
 the caller has to say which equality it meant. A 4-ULP ~ would call a
 nearly-coincident pair of path points coincident and swing that post to the fixed
 fallback direction instead of its own -- a visible wrong answer, not a rounding
 one. Same call as the hull sort in Tree, for the same reason.

  post-box : RiderPt, Real, Real -> RailPoly
  post-box (p) (ox) (ofwd) =
    let back-foot = Vec3 { right = p.right - ox, forward = p.forward - ofwd, height = 0.0 }
    in let fore-foot = Vec3 { right = p.right + ox, forward = p.forward + ofwd, height = 0.0 }
    in let fore-head = Vec3 { right = p.right + ox, forward = p.forward + ofwd, height = bar-top }
    in let back-head = Vec3 { right = p.right - ox, forward = p.forward - ofwd, height = bar-top }
    in rail-poly back-foot fore-foot fore-head back-head rail-post-metal

  post-quad : List RiderPt, Integer -> RailPoly
  post-quad (path) (i) =
    let n = list-length path
    in let ia = if i == 0 then 0 else i - 1
    in let ib = if i + 1 >= n then n - 1 else i + 1
    in let a = list-at path ia
    in let b = list-at path ib
    in let dr = b.right - a.right
    in let df = b.forward - a.forward
    in let raw = real-sqrt (dr * dr + df * df)
    in let run = if raw ~0 0.0 then 1.0 else raw
    in let ox = dr / run * half-post
    in let ofwd = df / run * half-post
    in post-box (list-at path i) ox ofwd

  posts : List RiderPt, Integer -> List RailPoly
  posts (path) (i) =
    if i >= list-length path then []
    else [post-quad path i] & posts path (i + 1)

Section: Emit

 Raise the ground path -- its centreline in the rider frame, one post per point --
 into the bar and its posts. A path of fewer than two points has no run direction
 and raises nothing.

  rail-emit : List RiderPt -> List RailPoly
  rail-emit (path) =
    if list-length path < 2 then []
    else list-take ((bars path 0) & posts path 0) max-rail-polys

Section: Draw

 Near-clip one stored rail poly, project it, and push the fill -- the same pipeline
 as a road quad, but the corners carry their own heights so it stands above the
 pavement. Called by render in depth-sorted order.

  project-all : List Vec3, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  project-all (ps) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [project (list-at ps i) cf view-w] & project-all ps cf view-w (i + 1)

  rail-draw-poly : RailPoly, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  rail-draw-poly (rp) (cf) (view-w) =
    let clipped = clip-near rp.v near
    in if list-length clipped < 3 then []
    else push-poly rp.color (project-all clipped cf view-w 0)

 A convenience for a caller that already holds the polys in the order it wants them
 painted. THE ORDER IS THE CALLER'S: render merges rails into one depth sort with
 the trees and the critters, and this walks the list exactly as given.

  rail-draw-all : List RailPoly, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  rail-draw-all (ps) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else (rail-draw-poly (list-at ps i) cf view-w) & rail-draw-all ps cf view-w (i + 1)

Chapter: SafariCritter
  cites Safari chapter World

 The two big animals parked AT a corner: an ADULT standing just past the far
 (outer) edge as if it had crossed, facing into the turn, and its BABY ahead in
 the rider's path beyond the turn.

 A COMPLETE port of wasm/safari_critter.zig, and it is worth saying why a whole
 file ports here when its neighbours arrive in halves. This one is placement and
 nothing else -- three constants, a species table, and one arithmetic rule the two
 animals share. WHAT DRAWS the pair is critter.zig, over emoji_frames.zig's 182 KB
 of baked polygons, and that is a different file, a different chapter, and the
 same deferral cat.zig already carries.

 THE POND IS THE EXCEPTION, and it is why this returns a LIST rather than a pair.
 A duck pond is a hand-drawn scene -- Safari chapter Pond, water and bank and
 ducks -- not an emoji pair, so it contributes no critters at all and render draws
 it separately. The terminus has no exit corner and contributes none either. The
 zig says that with an optional Species and a returned count; Codex has no null,
 so the count is the list's own length, and it is two or zero and never one.

 The late-route giant upsizing of safari_critter.ts is absent from the zig --
 Steve: never effective -- so it is absent here. The adult/baby sizing is kept.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

 Codex has no null, so the species table answers for every creature and `present`
 says whether the answer means anything. The same shape Gaze uses for the pig it
 may or may not have noticed.

  Species = record {
    present : Boolean,
    cp : Integer,
    adult-h : Real
  }

  no-species : Species
  no-species = Species { present = False, cp = 0, adult-h = 0.0 }

 The adult stands this much further off than half its own width, which is what
 keeps it clear of the guard rail rather than straddling it.

  adult-rail-buffer : Real
  adult-rail-buffer = 1.5

 The baby's height as a fraction of the adult, and how far past the turn it stands
 in the rider's path.

  baby-ratio : Real
  baby-ratio = 0.5

  baby-beyond : Real
  baby-beyond = 14.0

 The emoji and the adult height in metres, one row per species. The giraffe stands
 tallest. The rhino is about thirty percent smaller than the elephant on purpose:
 less of it blends into the sky, and it reads against the mountains instead.

  species-of : Creature -> Species
  species-of (c) =
   when c
    is Elephant -> Species { present = True, cp = 128024, adult-h = 2.8 }
    is Giraffe -> Species { present = True, cp = 129426, adult-h = 4.5 }
    is Zebra -> Species { present = True, cp = 129427, adult-h = 1.6 }
    is Rhino -> Species { present = True, cp = 129423, adult-h = 2.2 }
    is DuckPond -> no-species
    is NoCreature -> no-species

Section: The Corner Pair

 Both animals in the FROM segment's frame: `along` measured from that segment's
 start, `across` centre-relative, exactly as the trees and cows are authored.

 THE ADULT SITS ON THE SIDE AWAY FROM THE TURN -- the turn sign is negated -- so a
 right turn parks it left and a left turn parks it right. That is the OUTER corner,
 the one the rider swings widest around and looks straight at through the bend.
 Both animals face INTO the turn, so their eyes lead him around it.

  corner-critters : Creature, Real, Boolean, Real -> List Critter
  corner-critters (c) (along) (turn-right) (hw) =
    let sp = species-of c
    in let turn-sign = if turn-right then 1.0 else 0.0 - 1.0
    in let adult-h = sp.adult-h
    in if sp.present
      then [Critter { along = along, across = (0.0 - turn-sign) * (hw + adult-rail-buffer + adult-h / 2.0), codepoint = sp.cp, height = adult-h, face-right = turn-right },
            Critter { along = along + baby-beyond, across = 0.0, codepoint = sp.cp, height = adult-h * baby-ratio, face-right = turn-right }]
      else []

Chapter: Render
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Pond
  cites Safari chapter Tower
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Safari chapter Cat
  cites Safari chapter SafariCritter

 The frame mapping: which segments are in view, and how a point authored in any of
 their frames reaches the rider's.

 A partial port of wasm/render.zig -- the spine, not yet the frame. render.zig is
 the orchestrator: one 431-line `frame` that walks the chain, lays the road strip
 and each corner's pavement, collects the trees, towers, critters, cats, rails and
 the truck, depth-sorts the lot and draws it back to front. Every one of those
 steps stands on `at (d, a, x)` -- the composition ported here -- so this is the
 piece the rest of the module has to be built on.

 WHAT IS PORTED HERE IS SHAPED BY WHAT RENDER.ZIG LETS ANYTHING SEE, and that is a
 finding rather than a preference. Its entire public surface is four names --
 Chain, Pose, at, and frame. buildChain, Mapper, mapPt, and all four ground and
 rail emitters are private to the file. So `at` is the ONE piece of this chapter
 with a direct oracle: a probe can call it and compare. The chain and the prev
 mapper are graded against what the world and geom already expose, and RenderCheck
 says exactly where that is weaker and why. PORTING_NOTES E1 records it as the
 module's shape, not a gap in the check.

 We say:

Section: The Chain

 How many segments ahead are drawn, and the fixed buffer that bounds them. The zig
 carries both because its Chain is a fixed array that the look-ahead has to fit
 inside; the port keeps both because the SMALLER of the two is what actually stops
 the walk, and which one that is should stay visible rather than being folded into
 one number.

  look-ahead : Integer
  look-ahead = 7

  max-chain : Integer
  max-chain = 8

 THE CHAIN IS A LIST OF SEGMENT INDICES. The zig's Chain is an array plus a length
 because it has to be; a Codex list carries its own length, so the record collapses
 to the list and every `ch.len` becomes `list-length ch`.

 Follow exit-to from `start`, stopping at the cap or at the terminus -- no segment
 leads out of the finish line, so the chain ends there rather than looping. `n` is
 how many indices are already down, which is the zig's `ch.len` read at the TOP of
 its loop: the cap is tested before the index is appended, so a chain is never
 longer than the cap and the terminus is never dropped for being one past it.

  build-chain : List Segment, Integer -> List Integer
  build-chain (segs) (start) = chain-from segs start 0

  chain-from : List Segment, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  chain-from (segs) (s) (n) =
    if n >= look-ahead then []
    else if n >= max-chain then []
    else if (list-at segs s).terminates then [s]
    else [s] & chain-from segs (list-at segs s).exit-to (n + 1)

Section: The Rider Pose

 Where the rider sits on his CURRENT segment and which way he is looking. `hw` is
 that segment's half width, and it is here rather than being looked up because his
 `across` is centre-relative while to-rider wants from-the-left -- the same shift
 Camera's port already threads.

 The `yaw` handed in is not his heading alone. render.zig folds the distracted
 gaze and the lean's head turn into it before building the pose, so the whole view
 swings together with the backdrop; the port takes the sum, as the zig does.

  Pose = record {
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    yaw : Real,
    hw : Real
  }

Section: The Frame Composition

 at maps (a, x) in chain[d]'s bottom-left frame into the rider's, composing the
 joins down to chain[0] and then applying the rider transform. For d = 0 there is
 nothing to compose and it is just to-rider.

 THE JOINS COMPOSE BACKWARDS, from d down to 1, and the segment that supplies each
 join is the one BELOW it. chain[k] leads to chain[k+1] through chain[k]'s OWN exit
 turn, so stepping a point from chain[k+1]'s frame down into chain[k]'s reads
 chain[k]'s length, angle, direction and width -- never chain[k+1]'s. Off by one
 here and a distant segment lands rotated by its neighbour's turn, which does not
 look like corruption; it looks like a plausible road bending the wrong way.

  compose-down : List Segment, List Integer, Integer, Real, Real -> AX
  compose-down (segs) (ch) (k) (a) (x) =
    if k <= 0 then AX { a = a, x = x }
    else let seg = list-at segs (list-at ch (k - 1))
    in let p = next-to-cur a x seg.length seg.exit-angle seg.exit-right seg.width
    in compose-down segs ch (k - 1) p.a p.x

  at : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> RiderPt
  at (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (a) (x) =
    let p = compose-down segs ch d a x
    in to-rider p.a p.x pose.along pose.across pose.yaw pose.hw

Section: Mappers

 How to express a point authored in SOME segment's frame in the rider's. A forward
 joint maps both of its segments through the chain -- `at` at index d, above.

 THE JOINT JUST BEHIND THE RIDER IS THE ONE THAT CANNOT, and it is the reason this
 abstraction exists at all. Its `from` is the PREVIOUS segment, which is not on the
 chain: the chain starts at the rider's current segment and only ever runs forward.
 So a point authored there steps FORWARD through the join with cur-to-next -- the
 inverse of what every other point does -- and then goes straight to the rider.
 Without that pass the pavement the rider is still crossing vanishes the instant
 his segment index flips, which is the bug the behind-joint pass was written for
 and which the tower and the corner creatures each had in turn.

 The zig's Mapper is a struct with a two-valued tag; the port keeps the struct and
 narrows the tag to a Boolean, because a Codex variant carries no payload and the
 four prev fields have to live somewhere. `is-chain` reads as the zig's
 `kind == .chain`.

  Mapper = record {
    is-chain : Boolean,
    d : Integer,
    prev-len : Real,
    prev-angle : Real,
    prev-right : Boolean,
    prev-w : Real
  }

 The zig defaults every Mapper field and fills in the one or two that matter. Codex
 records are built whole, so the two constructors below take that place -- and they
 also make the two kinds impossible to half-build, which the defaults did not.

  chain-map : Integer -> Mapper
  chain-map (d) =
    Mapper { is-chain = True, d = d, prev-len = 0.0, prev-angle = 0.0, prev-right = False, prev-w = 0.0 }

  prev-map : Segment -> Mapper
  prev-map (s) =
    Mapper { is-chain = False, d = 0, prev-len = s.length, prev-angle = s.exit-angle, prev-right = s.exit-right, prev-w = s.width }

  map-pt : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real -> RiderPt
  map-pt (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (a) (x) =
    if m.is-chain
      then at segs ch pose m.d a x
      else let p = cur-to-next a x m.prev-len m.prev-angle m.prev-right m.prev-w
        in to-rider p.a p.x pose.along pose.across pose.yaw pose.hw

Section: Collection Limits and Culls

 Beyond this depth a tree draws as flat triangles rather than cone hulls, and the
 crown's foliage gradient fades to solid over this much depth. Both are drawing
 decisions, carried here because the collector is what measures the depth.

  detail-dist : Real
  detail-dist = 200.0

  crown-shade-dist : Real
  crown-shade-dist = 80.0

 Scenery shorter than this on screen is skipped.

  min-scenery-px : Real
  min-scenery-px = 2.0

  min-critter-px : Real
  min-critter-px = 2.0

 THE TWO CULLS ARE DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT AND BOTH MUST STAY LIVE. The SEGMENT
 cull saves projecting a critter at all: trees hide the farm animals past a few
 segments, and the corner safari animals carry a little further before they are
 worth it. The SIZE cull then trims whatever is left down to a genuine speck. The
 zig counts each separately every frame so a gate can assert both still fire -- a
 reach so loose the size cull never fires, or so tight the segments do all the
 work, is a bug in the budget. Those two counters are `pub`, and they are this
 chapter's only observable output.

  farm-seg-reach : Integer
  farm-seg-reach = 3

  safari-seg-reach : Integer
  safari-seg-reach = 5

 THE SIZE CULL MEASURES AGAINST THE STABLE BASE FOCAL, NOT THE LIVE ONE, and that
 is not an oversight to tidy up. The focal pulls in to 0.35x during a lean, so
 culling a critter on the live focal would yank close animals out of view the
 instant the rider turns and pop them back when he straightened. Scenery -- trees
 and the cat -- does cull on the live `cam-focal`. The two are different on
 purpose and the port keeps them different.

 The fixed-array caps. Codex lists carry their own length, so these bound nothing
 here; they are ported because they are observable IF reached, and the truncation
 below is where that would show. On this route they are not reached -- render's
 own note puts the tree peak at about 37 percent of its cap -- so the port
 truncates at the end where the zig stops mid-segment. Those differ only once a
 cap is hit, and making them agree there would mean threading a running count
 through every collector for a case this route cannot produce.

  max-vis-trees : Integer
  max-vis-trees = 640

  max-vis-towers : Integer
  max-vis-towers = 16

  max-vis-critters : Integer
  max-vis-critters = 320

  max-vis-cats : Integer
  max-vis-cats = 8

Section: Tower Placement

 Out past the corner, off to the right, and yawed. A long segment also stands its
 own tower halfway down, that far LEFT of the centreline.

  tower-beyond : Real
  tower-beyond = 160.0

  tower-right : Real
  tower-right = 20.0

  seg-tower-left : Real
  seg-tower-left = 100.0

 THE ZIG WRITES ITS OWN PI HERE, TRUNCATED, and the port copies the literal rather
 than reaching for Trig's `deg`. render.zig says `30.0 * 3.14159265 / 180.0`, which
 is 0.523598775 where thirty times a full-precision degree is 0.5235987756. The
 difference is below f32 and would almost certainly vanish at the seam -- but a
 port that silently improves a constant is a port whose disagreements no longer
 mean what they say, so the truncation is kept.

  tower-yaw : Real
  tower-yaw = 30.0 * 3.14159265 / 180.0

Section: The Collected Kinds

 The zig collects into PARALLEL ARRAYS -- t-right, t-fwd, t-h, t-col -- because it
 has to; the port collects records, which is the same data with the index made
 implicit. What the port does NOT change is that each kind is collected into its
 own list and merged only at the sort, because that is what makes the sort one
 pass over everything rather than a sort per kind.

  TreeItem = record {
    right : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    height : Real,
    color : Integer
  }

 A tower is collected as the RECIPE for its base rather than as points: the mapper
 that owns its frame, its base centre and yaw, and the beacon's blink offset. It
 has to be, because the four base corners are re-derived at draw time from the
 yaw. `fwd` is the depth of the centre, which is what it sorts by.

  TowerItem = record {
    map : Mapper,
    a0 : Real,
    x0 : Real,
    yaw : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    off : Real
  }

 Every emoji animal -- cows, bulls, the pig herd, the corner safari pair, the pond
 ducks -- collects to the same billboard, which is why one list holds all of them.

  Billboard = record {
    right : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    height : Real,
    cp : Integer,
    face-right : Boolean
  }

 A placement carries its own verdict rather than being dropped, so the two culls
 can be counted separately. `kept` says it survived both; `size-culled` says which
 one rejected it. The near-plane cull is SILENT in the zig -- it `continue`s
 without touching a counter -- so a placement can be neither kept nor size-culled,
 and that third state is the one the port has to preserve.

  Placed = record {
    b : Billboard,
    kept : Boolean,
    size-culled : Boolean
  }

Section: The Crossing Cat

 A cat is a billboard like the animals, but its across is not authored -- it comes
 out of the crossing clock, keyed on the rider's ROAD-ALONG GAP to it. So it needs
 something none of the other kinds do: how far down the route the chain segment it
 stands on begins.

 That gap starts NEGATIVE. The rider is already `along` into his own segment, so
 the start of chain[0] is behind him, and each further segment adds its length.
 The zig accumulates it as it walks; the port sums it, which is the same number
 and does not need the walk to carry state.

  chain-gap : List Segment, List Integer, Real, Integer -> Real
  chain-gap (w) (ch) (along) (d) =
    if d <= 0 then 0.0 - along
    else chain-gap w ch along (d - 1) + (list-at w (list-at ch (d - 1))).length

  CatItem = record {
    right : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    height : Real,
    pose-idx : Integer,
    lift : Real
  }

 THE CAT CULLS ON THE LIVE FOCAL, like the trees and unlike the critters, and it
 culls only at the near plane besides. render.zig argues the point: it is a flat
 billboard at one depth, so as the rider passes, the whole still scales and slides
 off to the left together -- it never streaks across the viewport the way a tower
 can -- and culling it earlier would vanish the collapsed cat exactly as he
 reaches it, instead of letting him pass it.

  cat-item : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Segment, Real -> List CatItem
  cat-item (w) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (gap) (sg) (v) =
    let st = cat-state sg.cat (gap + sg.cat.along) v
    in let rp = at w ch pose d sg.cat.along (st.across + sg.width / 2.0)
    in if rp.forward <= near then []
      else if sg.cat.height / rp.forward * cf < min-scenery-px then []
      else [CatItem { right = rp.right, fwd = rp.forward, height = sg.cat.height, pose-idx = st.pose-idx, lift = st.lift }]

  seg-cat : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Real -> List CatItem
  seg-cat (w) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (along) (v) =
    let sg = list-at w (list-at ch d)
    in if sg.has-cat then cat-item w ch pose d cf (chain-gap w ch along d) sg v else []

  walk-cats : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List CatItem
  walk-cats (w) (ch) (pose) (cf) (along) (v) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-cat w ch pose d cf along v) & walk-cats w ch pose cf along v (d + 1)

Section: Placing Billboards

 One rule for every emoji animal: map it into the rider frame, drop it if it is
 behind the near plane, size-cull it if it would project shorter than the floor.

  no-billboard : Billboard
  no-billboard = Billboard { right = 0.0, fwd = 0.0, height = 0.0, cp = 0, face-right = False }

  verdict : RiderPt, Real, Integer, Boolean -> Placed
  verdict (rp) (h) (cp) (fr) =
    if rp.forward <= near
      then Placed { b = no-billboard, kept = False, size-culled = False }
      else if h / rp.forward * focal < min-critter-px
        then Placed { b = no-billboard, kept = False, size-culled = True }
        else Placed { b = Billboard { right = rp.right, fwd = rp.forward, height = h, cp = cp, face-right = fr }, kept = True, size-culled = False }

  place-critter : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Critter -> Placed
  place-critter (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) (cr) =
    let rp = at segs ch pose d cr.along (cr.across + hw)
    in verdict rp cr.height cr.codepoint cr.face-right

 The behind joint's critters map FORWARD through the join instead, so they take
 the mapper rather than a chain index. Same verdict.

  place-critter-via : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Critter -> Placed
  place-critter-via (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (hw) (cr) =
    let rp = map-pt segs ch pose m cr.along (cr.across + hw)
    in verdict rp cr.height cr.codepoint cr.face-right

 A DUCK IS AUTHORED IN THE CORNER FRAME AND MAPS RAW -- no half-width shift. Its
 `cu` is the bottom-left across directly, which is why the pond's negative cu sits
 LEFT of the left edge rather than left of the centre line. Getting this wrong
 would slide the whole pond half a lane and still look like a pond.

  place-duck : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Duck -> Placed
  place-duck (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (dk) =
    let rp = map-pt segs ch pose m (from-len + dk.p.cv) dk.p.cu
    in verdict rp duck-height duck-codepoint dk.face-right

Section: Harvesting a Placement List

 Collect the survivors and count the size-culled, over a list of placements. Two
 walks over one list rather than one walk returning a pair, because a pair would
 need a record whose only purpose is to be taken apart again.

  kept-of : List Placed, Integer -> List Billboard
  kept-of (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else if (list-at ps i).kept then [(list-at ps i).b] & kept-of ps (i + 1)
    else kept-of ps (i + 1)

  size-culled-of : List Placed, Integer -> Integer
  size-culled-of (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else if (list-at ps i).size-culled then 1 + size-culled-of ps (i + 1)
    else size-culled-of ps (i + 1)

  place-all : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, List Critter, Integer -> List Placed
  place-all (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) (crs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length crs then []
    else [place-critter segs ch pose d hw (list-at crs i)] & place-all segs ch pose d hw crs (i + 1)

  place-all-via : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, List Critter, Integer -> List Placed
  place-all-via (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (hw) (crs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length crs then []
    else [place-critter-via segs ch pose m hw (list-at crs i)] & place-all-via segs ch pose m hw crs (i + 1)

  place-ducks : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Integer -> List Placed
  place-ducks (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ducks then []
    else [place-duck segs ch pose m from-len (list-at ducks i)] & place-ducks segs ch pose m from-len (i + 1)

Section: Collecting Trees

 Trees are authored centre-relative, so the half width shifts them to
 from-the-left. They cull on the LIVE focal, unlike the critters above.

  place-tree : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Tree -> List TreeItem
  place-tree (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (hw) (tr) =
    let rp = at segs ch pose d tr.along (tr.across + hw)
    in if rp.forward <= near then []
      else if tr.height / rp.forward * cf < min-scenery-px then []
      else [TreeItem { right = rp.right, fwd = rp.forward, height = tr.height, color = tr.color }]

  seg-trees : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, List Tree, Integer -> List TreeItem
  seg-trees (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (hw) (trs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length trs then []
    else (place-tree segs ch pose d cf hw (list-at trs i)) & seg-trees segs ch pose d cf hw trs (i + 1)

Section: Collecting Towers

 A tower is collected only if its base CENTRE is in front of the near plane, and
 the centre is what it sorts by. The exit tower is keyed off its own segment; the
 mid-tower adds sixty so a long segment's two towers do not blink in step.

  tower-if-ahead : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List TowerItem
  tower-if-ahead (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (a0) (x0) (yw) (key) =
    let c = map-pt segs ch pose m a0 x0
    in if c.forward <= near then []
      else [TowerItem { map = m, a0 = a0, x0 = x0, yaw = yw, fwd = c.forward, off = beacon-offset-for key }]

  seg-towers : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  seg-towers (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in (tower-if-ahead segs ch pose (chain-map d) (sg.length + tower-beyond) (sg.width / 2.0 + tower-right) tower-yaw (list-at ch d))
       & (seg-mid-tower segs ch pose d)

  seg-mid-tower : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  seg-mid-tower (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if sg.has-mid-tower
      then tower-if-ahead segs ch pose (chain-map d) (sg.length / 2.0) (sg.width / 2.0 - seg-tower-left) 0.0 (list-at ch d + 60)
      else []

Section: Collecting Critters

  is-pond : Creature -> Boolean
  is-pond (c) =
   when c
    is DuckPond -> True
    is NoCreature -> False
    is Elephant -> False
    is Giraffe -> False
    is Zebra -> False
    is Rhino -> False

 The farm animals of one chain segment, if it is near enough to be worth
 projecting at all. Cows first, then the pig herd -- the order the zig fills its
 array in, which is the order ties break in at the sort.

  seg-farm : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real -> List Placed
  seg-farm (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if d >= farm-seg-reach then []
      else (place-all segs ch pose d hw sg.cows 0) & (place-all segs ch pose d hw sg.pigs 0)

 The SEGMENT cull's tally: every farm animal on a chain segment beyond the reach,
 counted without being placed. This is the counter that makes the chain's own
 extent observable from outside render.zig, because it sums over exactly the chain
 indices at or past the reach.

  seg-cull-count : List Segment, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  seg-cull-count (segs) (ch) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if d >= farm-seg-reach then list-length sg.cows + list-length sg.pigs else 0

 The corner pair at this segment's exit. Skipped at the terminus, which has no
 exit corner, and beyond the safari reach.

  seg-safari : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real -> List Placed
  seg-safari (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if sg.terminates then []
      else if d >= safari-seg-reach then []
      else place-all segs ch pose d hw (corner-critters sg.exit-creature sg.length sg.exit-right hw) 0

 The three ducks on the pond at this segment's exit. The pond's WATER and BANK are
 ground quads and belong to the paint pass, not to this one; only the billboards
 are collected for the sort.

  seg-ducks : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  seg-ducks (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if d >= safari-seg-reach then []
      else if is-pond sg.exit-creature then place-ducks segs ch pose (chain-map d) sg.length 0
      else []

 Everything one chain segment contributes to the billboard list, in the zig's own
 fill order: farm animals, then the corner pair, then the ducks.

  seg-billboards : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  seg-billboards (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let hw = (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width / 2.0
    in (seg-farm segs ch pose d hw) & (seg-safari segs ch pose d hw) & (seg-ducks segs ch pose d)

Section: Walking the Chain

  walk-trees : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Integer -> List TreeItem
  walk-trees (segs) (ch) (pose) (cf) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-trees segs ch pose d cf ((list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width / 2.0) (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).trees 0)
         & walk-trees segs ch pose cf (d + 1)

  walk-towers : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  walk-towers (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-towers segs ch pose d) & walk-towers segs ch pose (d + 1)

  walk-billboards : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  walk-billboards (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-billboards segs ch pose d) & walk-billboards segs ch pose (d + 1)

  walk-seg-cull : List Segment, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  walk-seg-cull (segs) (ch) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then 0
    else seg-cull-count segs ch d + walk-seg-cull segs ch (d + 1)

Section: The Joint Just Behind

 The segment the rider just left owns a corner, and everything on it -- pavement,
 tower, creatures, pond -- pops out the instant his segment index flips unless it
 is drawn through the join. The route is finite, so there is a previous segment
 everywhere EXCEPT the start.

 THE BEHIND CRITTERS TAKE NO REACH TEST. The forward ones are gated on
 safari-seg-reach; this pair is one join away by construction, so the zig does not
 test it and neither does the port.

  behind-billboards : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  behind-billboards (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in (place-all-via segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (pv.width / 2.0) (corner-critters pv.exit-creature pv.length pv.exit-right (pv.width / 2.0)) 0)
       & (behind-ducks segs ch pose pv)

  behind-ducks : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Segment -> List Placed
  behind-ducks (segs) (ch) (pose) (pv) =
    if is-pond pv.exit-creature then place-ducks segs ch pose (prev-map pv) pv.length 0 else []

  behind-tower : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  behind-tower (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in tower-if-ahead segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (pv.length + tower-beyond) (pv.width / 2.0 + tower-right) tower-yaw prev-idx

Section: The Corner's Outer Shoulders

 Both the corner pavement and the guard rail hang off the same three points: each
 segment's OUTER shoulder at the join, and the apex Q where the two extended
 shoulders cross. Which edge is outer flips with the turn -- a right turn's outer
 edge is the left one.

  outer-cu : Boolean, Real -> Real
  outer-cu (exit-right) (wd) = if exit-right then 0.0 else wd

 Q is found by extending each shoulder one metre past the join and intersecting
 the two lines. A real turn has a non-zero angle, so they are never parallel.

  joint-apex : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> RiderPt
  joint-apex (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    let fcu = outer-cu exit-right from-w
    in let tx = outer-cu exit-right to-w
    in line-meet (map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len fcu) (map-pt segs ch pose from-map (from-len + 1.0) fcu) (map-pt segs ch pose to-map 0.0 tx) (map-pt segs ch pose to-map 1.0 tx)

Section: The Guard Rail Path

 Append points from `from` EXCLUSIVE to `to` inclusive at about one metre spacing
 -- one rail post per metre along a straight leg of the corner. At least one step
 always, so a zero-length leg still lands its endpoint.

  leg-steps : Real -> Integer
  leg-steps (dist) =
    let r = round-real dist
    in real-to-int (if r < 1.0 then 1.0 else r)

  leg-points : RiderPt, Real, Real, Integer, Integer -> List RiderPt
  leg-points (from) (dr) (df) (steps) (i) =
    if i > steps then []
    else let t = real-from-int i / real-from-int steps
    in [RiderPt { right = from.right + dr * t, forward = from.forward + df * t }] & leg-points from dr df steps (i + 1)

  push-leg : RiderPt, RiderPt -> List RiderPt
  push-leg (from) (to) =
    let dr = to.right - from.right
    in let df = to.forward - from.forward
    in leg-points from dr df (leg-steps (real-sqrt (dr * dr + df * df))) 1

 The run-up counts DOWN to the end edge, so the last point of the run-up is the
 outer corner itself and the first leg starts from it.

  rail-run-up : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real, Integer -> List RiderPt
  rail-run-up (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (cu) (k) =
    if k < 0 then []
    else [map-pt segs ch pose m (from-len - real-from-int k) cu] & rail-run-up segs ch pose m from-len cu (k - 1)

  rail-run-out : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Integer -> List RiderPt
  rail-run-out (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (x) (k) =
    if k > rail-runout then []
    else [map-pt segs ch pose m (real-from-int k) x] & rail-run-out segs ch pose m x (k + 1)

 The whole corner path: run-up along the from segment's outer shoulder, the two
 legs into and out of the apex, and the run-out along the to segment's. guard_rail
 then raises that path to a bar strip and its posts.

  joint-rail-path : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List RiderPt
  joint-rail-path (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    let fcu = outer-cu exit-right from-w
    in let tx = outer-cu exit-right to-w
    in let of-pt = map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len fcu
    in let ot-pt = map-pt segs ch pose to-map 0.0 tx
    in let q = joint-apex segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right
    in (rail-run-up segs ch pose from-map from-len fcu rail-runout) & (push-leg of-pt q) & (push-leg q ot-pt) & (rail-run-out segs ch pose to-map tx 1)

  joint-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List RailPoly
  joint-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    rail-emit (joint-rail-path segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right)

 Every FORWARD joint on the chain, in chain order, then the joint behind. That is
 the order the zig's rail store holds them in, and the order ties break in.

  walk-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List RailPoly
  walk-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d + 1 >= list-length ch then []
    else (joint-rails segs ch pose (chain-map d) (chain-map (d + 1)) (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width (list-at segs (list-at ch (d + 1))).width (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).exit-right)
         & walk-rails segs ch pose (d + 1)

  behind-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List RailPoly
  behind-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in joint-rails segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (chain-map 0) pv.length pv.width (list-at segs (list-at ch 0)).width pv.exit-right

Section: The Chased Truck

 Walk the truck's LEAD -- its route distance minus the rider's -- forward along
 the chain to find which segment its centre lands on. It sorts at that centre-line
 depth, one item however long the body is.

  TruckAt = record {
    present : Boolean,
    d : Integer,
    along : Real,
    fwd : Real
  }

  no-truck : TruckAt
  no-truck = TruckAt { present = False, d = 0, along = 0.0, fwd = 0.0 }

  truck-step : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Integer -> TruckAt
  truck-step (segs) (ch) (pose) (remaining) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then no-truck
    else if remaining > (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length
      then truck-step segs ch pose (remaining - (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length) (d + 1)
      else truck-here segs ch pose remaining d

  truck-here : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Integer -> TruckAt
  truck-here (segs) (ch) (pose) (remaining) (d) =
    let c = at segs ch pose d remaining ((list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width / 2.0)
    in if c.forward > near
      then TruckAt { present = True, d = d, along = remaining, fwd = c.forward }
      else no-truck

 A lead of zero or less means the truck is level with or behind the rider, and it
 is simply not drawn -- the zig does not clamp it forward.

  truck-at : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Real -> TruckAt
  truck-at (segs) (ch) (pose) (along) (lead) =
    if lead > 0.0 then truck-step segs ch pose (along + lead) 0 else no-truck

Section: The Depth Sort

 One order over everything that stands up off the ground, far to near, so a tree
 occludes a rail and a rail occludes a cow by honest depth rather than by paint
 order. This is what guard_rail's collect-then-sort design exists to feed, and why
 its RailPoly carries a `fwd` it never draws with.

  Kind =
   | KTree
   | KTower
   | KCow
   | KCat
   | KTruck
   | KRail

  Item = record {
    fwd : Real,
    kind : Kind,
    i : Integer
  }

  tree-items : List TreeItem, Integer -> List Item
  tree-items (ts) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ts then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at ts i).fwd, kind = KTree, i = i }] & tree-items ts (i + 1)

  tower-items : List TowerItem, Integer -> List Item
  tower-items (ts) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ts then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at ts i).fwd, kind = KTower, i = i }] & tower-items ts (i + 1)

  cow-items : List Billboard, Integer -> List Item
  cow-items (bs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length bs then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at bs i).fwd, kind = KCow, i = i }] & cow-items bs (i + 1)

  cat-items : List CatItem, Integer -> List Item
  cat-items (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at cs i).fwd, kind = KCat, i = i }] & cat-items cs (i + 1)

  rail-items : List RailPoly, Integer -> List Item
  rail-items (rs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length rs then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at rs i).fwd, kind = KRail, i = i }] & rail-items rs (i + 1)

  truck-items : TruckAt -> List Item
  truck-items (t) = if t.present then [Item { fwd = t.fwd, kind = KTruck, i = 0 }] else []

 THE SORT MUST BE STABLE AND THE ZIG'S IS. Its insertion loop moves a key left
 only while the element before it is STRICTLY nearer-to-far -- `items[j-1].fwd <
 key.fwd` -- so equal depths keep the order they were merged in. That matters more
 here than it looks: a rail bar and its post share a corner and can land on the
 same depth, and so can two roadside trees on opposite shoulders.

 `core/Sort` EXISTS AND MUST NOT BE SUBSTITUTED HERE, which is worth saying before
 the merge is described, because the shape of this chapter invites the swap. The
 foreword's `sort-by : List a, (a, a -> Integer) -> List a` looks like exactly what
 this wants and is an in-place QUICKSORT with median-of-three pivoting. Quicksort
 swaps across equal keys; it is not stable and its chapter claims no stability.

 STABILITY IS LOAD-BEARING HERE AND THE EVIDENCE IS A BUG THAT HAPPENED. Ties are
 real -- render.zig sorts by an f32 depth and equal depths keep merge order -- and
 PORTING_NOTES D9 records a rail post and a rail bar swapping places in a
 3,091-command stream, one adjacent transposition with every other command
 identical, invisible to every per-module check because both modules were right.
 Handing this to `sort-by` would reintroduce that class silently and only the
 whole-frame check would ever say so. De-duplicating against the foreword is right
 far more often than it is wrong; this is one of the times it is wrong.

 THE PORT SORTS BY MERGING RATHER THAN BY INSERTING, and that is the one place in
 this chapter where the algorithm departs from the zig on purpose. A stable merge
 sort and a stable insertion sort produce the SAME total order -- same comparison,
 same tie rule -- so nothing observable changes. What changes is the cost, and on
 persistent lists the difference is not a constant factor.

 Insertion sort on a mutable array shifts elements in place. On a Codex list there
 is nothing to shift: rebuilding the tail past the insertion point allocates a list
 per element, so ONE insert is quadratic in allocations and the whole sort is
 cubic. Measured, it is not slow but fatal -- seven hundred items across eight
 frames exhausted codexzig's whole 4 GiB bump heap, which never reclaims (C6). The
 merge is quadratic in copies and comes in around a hundred megabytes.

 So the rule this is an instance of: PORTING AN ALGORITHM FAITHFULLY MEANS PORTING
 ITS ANSWER, NOT ITS COST MODEL. An in-place algorithm carried literally onto
 persistent lists can gain a whole complexity class, and the bump heap turns that
 into a crash rather than a wait. See PORTING_NOTES E4.

  rest-from : List Item, Integer -> List Item
  rest-from (ys) (j) =
    if j >= list-length ys then []
    else [list-at ys j] & rest-from ys (j + 1)

 Merge two runs already in far-to-near order. `a` wins ties -- the test asks
 whether b is STRICTLY deeper -- which is what makes the merge stable and matches
 the zig's strict `<`.

  merge-items : List Item, List Item, Integer, Integer -> List Item
  merge-items (a) (b) (i) (j) =
    if i >= list-length a then rest-from b j
    else if j >= list-length b then rest-from a i
    else if deeper-than (list-at b j).fwd (list-at a i).fwd
      then [list-at b j] & merge-items a b i (j + 1)
      else [list-at a i] & merge-items a b (i + 1) j

 THE SORT DECLINES TO ORDER TWO DEPTHS IT CANNOT TELL APART, and that is the one
 place this chapter's comparison is not the zig's character for character. The
 zig's key is an f32; the port's is a Real, which is f64 in every plug. Two depths
 that differ by less than the f32 grid are THE SAME NUMBER in the game -- its sort
 sees a tie and keeps merge order -- while the port can see a difference and would
 order them by it. Sorting on precision the oracle does not have is not fidelity;
 it is the port inventing an order.

 So `deeper-than` is a strict comparison with half an f32 ulp of slack, and the
 slack is measured rather than chosen. A rail post and a rail bar in the whole
 frame check land within 6e-8 of each other and swap without it; at 2e-8 they
 still swap, so the gap is between a sixth and a half of an ulp. Half an ulp is
 the largest threshold that cannot tie two depths the game holds apart, since two
 distinct f32s differ by a whole one.

 A grader that measures inversions has to use the same rule -- RenderCheck's does,
 and its own prose says why. A property asserted at a finer resolution than the
 sort works at reports the rule as a defect.

  sort-tie : Real
  sort-tie = 0.00000006

  deeper-than : Real, Real -> Boolean
  deeper-than (x) (y) = x - y > sort-tie * real-max (real-abs y) 1.0

  sort-items : List Item -> List Item
  sort-items (xs) =
    if list-length xs <= 1 then xs
    else merge-items (sort-items (list-take xs (list-length xs / 2))) (sort-items (list-drop xs (list-length xs / 2))) 0 0

Section: The Frame's Collection

 Everything the chain walk gathers, plus the two cull tallies and the one depth
 order over the lot. What this does NOT carry is the ground -- the road strips,
 the corner pavement and the pond's water and bank are painted inside the same
 walk in the zig, but they are quads on the floor and never enter the sort, so
 they belong to the paint pass and are not ported here.

 ALL SEVEN KINDS ARE COLLECTED NOW. The cat was the last, and what unblocked it
 was not writing the logarithm its `pow(b, 0.7)` seemed to need: 0.75 is dyadic
 where 0.7 is not, so the front-loaded leap is a pair of square roots and the
 approximation is bounded at 8.6 cm for two frames. Safari chapter Cat argues it.

  Collected = record {
    trees : List TreeItem,
    towers : List TowerItem,
    cows : List Billboard,
    cats : List CatItem,
    rails : List RailPoly,
    truck : TruckAt,
    order : List Item,
    cull-seg : Integer,
    cull-size : Integer
  }

  prev-index : Integer -> Integer
  prev-index (seg-idx) = if seg-idx > 0 then seg-idx - 1 else 0

  all-towers : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  all-towers (segs) (ch) (pose) (seg-idx) =
    if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-towers segs ch pose 0) & (behind-tower segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1))
      else walk-towers segs ch pose 0

  all-placed : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  all-placed (segs) (ch) (pose) (seg-idx) =
    if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-billboards segs ch pose 0) & (behind-billboards segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1))
      else walk-billboards segs ch pose 0

  all-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List RailPoly
  all-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (seg-idx) =
    if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-rails segs ch pose 0) & (behind-rails segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1))
      else walk-rails segs ch pose 0

 The merge order is the zig's own array order -- trees, towers, billboards, the
 truck, then the rails -- and since the sort is stable it is also the order equal
 depths come out in.

  collect : List Segment, Integer, Pose, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Collected
  collect (segs) (seg-idx) (pose) (cf) (along) (v) (truck-pos) =
    let ch = build-chain segs seg-idx
    in let placed = all-placed segs ch pose seg-idx
    in let trees = list-take (walk-trees segs ch pose cf 0) max-vis-trees
    in let towers = list-take (all-towers segs ch pose seg-idx) max-vis-towers
    in let cows = list-take (kept-of placed 0) max-vis-critters
    in let cats = list-take (walk-cats segs ch pose cf along v 0) max-vis-cats
    in let rails = all-rails segs ch pose seg-idx
    in let tk = truck-at segs ch pose along (truck-pos - route-distance segs seg-idx along)
    in Collected {
      trees = trees,
      towers = towers,
      cows = cows,
      cats = cats,
      rails = rails,
      truck = tk,
      order = sort-items ((tree-items trees 0) & (tower-items towers 0) & (cow-items cows 0) & (cat-items cats 0) & (truck-items tk) & (rail-items rails 0)),
      cull-seg = walk-seg-cull segs ch 0,
      cull-size = size-culled-of placed 0
    }

Section: The Ground

 The road surface and everything paved: road strips, each corner's approach and
 pavement, and the pond's water and bank. These are quads on the FLOOR. They never
 enter the depth sort -- they are painted first, in walk order, and everything that
 stands up is sorted and painted over them.

  road-color : Integer
  road-color = 3421500

 Road strips are sliced this long so a bend reads smooth rather than as one long
 flat quad cutting the curve.

  road-chunk : Real
  road-chunk = 25.0

 Metres of approach road a joint paints behind its end edge. It is what covers the
 joint when the joint is the one BEHIND the rider.

  entry-road-dist : Real
  entry-road-dist = 40.0

 A ground vertex drops below the rider's tangent plane by the curvature at its
 horizontal distance, which is what bends the road toward a finite horizon instead
 of a vanishing point at infinity. The sign is the thing to get right: ground-drop
 returns a positive distance and the height is its NEGATIVE.

  ground-vert : RiderPt -> Vec3
  ground-vert (p) =
    Vec3 { right = p.right, forward = p.forward, height = 0.0 - ground-drop p.right p.forward }

  ground-verts : List RiderPt, Integer -> List Vec3
  ground-verts (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [ground-vert (list-at ps i)] & ground-verts ps (i + 1)

 THE EIGHT-POINT GUARD IS THE ZIG'S FIXED BUFFER AND IT IS KEPT. A caller handing
 in more than eight points gets nothing at all rather than a truncated polygon,
 which is the zig's behaviour and is the safer of the two: a silently shortened
 ground quad would be a wrong shape painted confidently.

  emit-ground-color : List RiderPt, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-ground-color (ps) (color) (cf) (view-w) =
    if list-length ps > 8 then []
    else ground-clip (clip-near (ground-verts ps 0) near) color cf view-w

  ground-clip : List Vec3, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  ground-clip (vs) (color) (cf) (view-w) =
    if list-length vs < 3 then []
    else push-poly color (project-all vs cf view-w 0)

  emit-ground : List RiderPt, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-ground (ps) (cf) (view-w) = emit-ground-color ps road-color cf view-w

Section: The Road Strip

 The zig takes the ceiling of length over the chunk size and floors it at one
 chunk. Codex's foreword has a floor and no ceiling, so this is the identity
 ceil x = -floor(-x) rather than a second rounding rule.

  ceil-real : Real -> Real
  ceil-real (x) = 0.0 - floor-real (0.0 - x)

  chunks-for : Real -> Integer
  chunks-for (len) =
    let c = ceil-real (len / road-chunk)
    in real-to-int (if c < 1.0 then 1.0 else c)

 EACH SLICE IS CUT FROM THE SEGMENT LENGTH BY INDEX, not accumulated by adding a
 chunk at a time. It matters: the zig computes a0 and a1 as length*i/n and
 length*(i+1)/n, so the last slice ends exactly on the segment end and the strip
 cannot drift short of the joint by an accumulated rounding.

  road-slice : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Integer, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  road-slice (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) (ci) (n) (len) (wd) =
    if ci >= n then []
    else (emit-ground (slice-quad segs ch pose d ci n len wd) cf view-w)
         & road-slice segs ch pose d cf view-w (ci + 1) n len wd

  slice-quad : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Integer, Integer, Real, Real -> List RiderPt
  slice-quad (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (ci) (n) (len) (wd) =
    let a0 = len * real-from-int ci / real-from-int n
    in let a1 = len * real-from-int (ci + 1) / real-from-int n
    in [at segs ch pose d a0 0.0, at segs ch pose d a0 wd, at segs ch pose d a1 wd, at segs ch pose d a1 0.0]

  seg-road : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-road (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in road-slice segs ch pose d cf view-w 0 (chunks-for sg.length) sg.length sg.width

Section: A Joint's Ground

 The approach road is the `from` segment's tail, its end edge drawn back
 entry-road-dist. The corner pavement is a quad from the INNER fuse-corner out to
 the outer apex Q: inner corner, `from`'s outer corner, Q, `to`'s outer corner.

 THE INNER CORNER IS THE EDGE THE TURN GOES TOWARD and the outer is the one it
 goes away from, so both flip with the turn direction and they flip oppositely.
 Getting the pair the wrong way round still paints a quad -- just one folded
 through the intersection.

  joint-approach : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real -> List RiderPt
  joint-approach (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (from-len) (from-w) =
    [map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len 0.0,
     map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len from-w,
     map-pt segs ch pose from-map (from-len - entry-road-dist) from-w,
     map-pt segs ch pose from-map (from-len - entry-road-dist) 0.0]

  joint-pavement : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List RiderPt
  joint-pavement (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    let inner = map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len (if exit-right then from-w else 0.0)
    in let outer-from = map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len (outer-cu exit-right from-w)
    in let outer-to = map-pt segs ch pose to-map 0.0 (outer-cu exit-right to-w)
    in let q = joint-apex segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right
    in [inner, outer-from, q, outer-to]

  emit-joint-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-joint-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) (cf) (view-w) =
    (emit-ground (joint-approach segs ch pose from-map from-len from-w) cf view-w)
    & (emit-ground (joint-pavement segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right) cf view-w)

Section: The Pond's Ground

 The water then the bank, authored in the incoming corner frame: corner (cu, cv)
 is map (from-len + cv, cu). The ducks that float on it are billboards and were
 collected for the depth sort; only the two ground shapes are painted here.

  pond-shape : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, List PondPt, Integer -> List RiderPt
  pond-shape (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [map-pt segs ch pose m (from-len + (list-at ps i).cv) (list-at ps i).cu] & pond-shape segs ch pose m from-len ps (i + 1)

  emit-pond-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-pond-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (cf) (view-w) =
    (emit-ground-color (pond-shape segs ch pose m from-len water-outline 0) water-color cf view-w)
    & (emit-ground-color (pond-shape segs ch pose m from-len bank 0) bank-color cf view-w)

Section: Walking the Ground

 One chain segment's floor: its road strip, then its exit joint's ground when the
 next segment is in view, then the pond at that exit if there is one. The pond
 ground takes NO reach test -- the ducks on it do, but the water is painted
 wherever the corner is drawn at all.

  seg-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    (seg-road segs ch pose d cf view-w)
    & (seg-joint-ground segs ch pose d cf view-w)
    & (seg-pond-ground segs ch pose d cf view-w)

  seg-joint-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-joint-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    if d + 1 >= list-length ch then []
    else emit-joint-ground segs ch pose (chain-map d) (chain-map (d + 1)) (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width (list-at segs (list-at ch (d + 1))).width (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).exit-right cf view-w

  seg-pond-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-pond-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if is-pond sg.exit-creature
      then emit-pond-ground segs ch pose (chain-map d) sg.length cf view-w
      else []

  walk-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  walk-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (cf) (view-w) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-ground segs ch pose d cf view-w) & walk-ground segs ch pose cf view-w (d + 1)

  behind-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  behind-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) (cf) (view-w) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in (emit-joint-ground segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (chain-map 0) pv.length pv.width (list-at segs (list-at ch 0)).width pv.exit-right cf view-w)
       & (behind-pond-ground segs ch pose pv cf view-w)

  behind-pond-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Segment, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  behind-pond-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (pv) (cf) (view-w) =
    if is-pond pv.exit-creature
      then emit-pond-ground segs ch pose (prev-map pv) pv.length cf view-w
      else []

 The whole floor for one frame, forward chain then the joint behind.

  frame-ground : List Segment, Integer, Pose, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-ground (segs) (seg-idx) (pose) (cf) (view-w) =
    let ch = build-chain segs seg-idx
    in if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-ground segs ch pose cf view-w 0) & (behind-ground segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1) cf view-w)
      else walk-ground segs ch pose cf view-w 0

Chapter: Pose

 The rider's state, and nothing else.

 THIS CHAPTER EXISTS TO CUT A CYCLE. In the zig, rider.zig imports gaze.zig for the
 gawk brake and the gaze step, and gaze.zig imports rider.zig for the state type --
 a mutual import zig permits and Codex cites do not. The cycle has to be cut
 somewhere, and the honest place is the thing they actually share: the record.
 Neither module's LOGIC moves, so this is a packaging adaptation and not a change
 of behaviour, in the same family as Camera threading view-w because Codex has no
 mutable global.

 A port of the RiderState in wasm/rider.zig.

 We say:

Section: State

 `yaw` is the heading against the CURRENT segment -- frame-relative, which is what
 the road geometry reads -- and is reset into each new segment's frame at a
 crossing.

 `heading` is the bike's ABSOLUTE world heading, integrated continuously and left
 untouched by the crossing. The backdrop reads this one, so the horizon turns
 exactly as much as the bike does rather than jumping when the frame changes.

 `gaze-yaw` and `focus` are the distracted rider, and both are VIEW-ONLY: an extra
 camera yaw toward the roadside pigs, and the camera's focus-narrowing progress as
 he gawks. They are advanced after the bike has moved.

  RiderState = record {
    segment : Integer,
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    yaw : Real,
    v : Real,
    tilt : Real,
    heading : Real,
    gaze-yaw : Real,
    focus : Real
  }

  v-base : Real
  v-base = 0.3

  initial-rider-state : RiderState
  initial-rider-state =
    RiderState { segment = 0, along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, v = v-base, tilt = 0.0, heading = 0.0, gaze-yaw = 0.0, focus = 0.0 }

Chapter: Gaze
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Pose
  cites Safari chapter World

 The distracted rider: his head swivels toward the roadside pigs and drifts back
 to the road, and his focus narrows while he gawks.

 A port of wasm/gaze.zig.

 BOTH OUTPUTS ARE VIEW-ONLY except one. `gaze-yaw` is an extra camera yaw and
 `focus` feeds the focal pull-in; neither moves the bike. The single reach into
 the motion is `pig-gaze-brake`, which the rider folds into its min-of-brakes --
 and that is the whole of the distraction's cost in metres.

 It takes the RiderState from `Safari chapter Pose` rather than from Rider,
 because the zig has rider.zig and gaze.zig importing each other and Codex cites
 cannot. See Pose for why the record is the right place to cut it.

 We say:

Section: Thresholds

  gaze-look-dist : Real
  gaze-look-dist = 150.0

 The gaze peaks here; once the pig swings this far off his heading he looks back.

  gaze-release-angle : Real
  gaze-release-angle = 35.0 * deg

 The most his head turns TOWARD the pig in one frame -- a gentle swivel -- against
 the slow, unhurried drift BACK to the road.

  gaze-swivel-rate : Real
  gaze-swivel-rate = 4.0 * deg

  gaze-return-rate : Real
  gaze-return-rate = 0.2 * deg

 Near straight the return step shrinks to this fraction of the remaining angle, so
 it decelerates in; below the snap angle the last sliver goes to zero, which keeps
 the focus trigger exact.

  gaze-return-ease : Real
  gaze-return-ease = 0.05

  gaze-return-snap : Real
  gaze-return-snap = 0.02 * deg

  focus-decay : Real
  focus-decay = 0.0012

 The slow gawk speed he eases to and then HOLDS for the rest of the segment, and
 how far before the pig he finishes slowing.

  pig-gaze-speed : Real
  pig-gaze-speed = 0.20

  pig-gaze-settle-dist : Real
  pig-gaze-settle-dist = 25.0

 Pointed more than this off the lane means mid-corner, and his eyes snap back to
 the road.

  eyes-on-road-yaw : Real
  eyes-on-road-yaw = 6.0 * deg

Section: Focus

  smoothstep : Real -> Real
  smoothstep (t) = t * t * (3.0 - 2.0 * t)

 state.focus is a LINEAR progress; this smoothsteps it so the narrow and the
 re-widen ease gently in and out.

  gaze-focus : Real -> Real
  gaze-focus (f) = smoothstep f

Section: Looking

 Is the designated pig still AHEAD and inside the looking window: a distraction
 leg, eyes not committed to a corner, the pig within the look distance, and not
 yet swung past the release angle off his heading.

 The zig returns an optional distance. Codex has no null, so the two answers are a
 record -- `looking` says whether there is any pig interest at all, and every
 caller checks it before reading `dist`.

  PigLook = record {
    looking : Boolean,
    dist : Real
  }

  no-look : PigLook
  no-look = PigLook { looking = False, dist = 0.0 }

  pig-ahead : RiderState, Segment -> PigLook
  pig-ahead (state) (seg) =
    if seg.pigs-distract == False then no-look
    else if real-abs state.yaw > eyes-on-road-yaw then no-look
    else pig-ahead-near state seg

  pig-ahead-near : RiderState, Segment -> PigLook
  pig-ahead-near (state) (seg) =
    let pig = gaze-pig seg.length (seg.width / 2.0)
    in let dist = pig.along - state.along
    in if dist > gaze-look-dist then no-look
    else pig-ahead-bearing state pig dist

  pig-ahead-bearing : RiderState, Critter, Real -> PigLook
  pig-ahead-bearing (state) (pig) (dist) =
    let bearing = r-atan2 (pig.across - state.across) dist - state.yaw
    in if real-abs bearing >= gaze-release-angle then no-look
       else PigLook { looking = True, dist = dist }

 Where he WANTS to look this frame: the bearing to the designated pig relative to
 his heading, or zero when he has no pig interest. A BEARING, not a fixed angle,
 so his gaze tracks the pig as he creeps past it.

  desired-gaze : RiderState, Segment -> Real
  desired-gaze (state) (seg) =
    if (pig-ahead state seg).looking == False then 0.0
    else desired-gaze-at state (gaze-pig seg.length (seg.width / 2.0))

  desired-gaze-at : RiderState, Critter -> Real
  desired-gaze-at (state) (pig) =
    r-atan2 (pig.across - state.across) (pig.along - state.along) - state.yaw

Section: The Gaze Step

 Run AFTER the bike has moved: swivel his head one frame toward where he wants to
 look. Toward the pig is a gentle fixed-rate swivel; the drift back to the road
 lingers, then decelerates into straight, snapping the last sliver to zero.

 The `want /= 0` test is a Real comparison the zig writes as `want != 0`, which is
 bitwise, so it is ~0 here and not ~. desired-gaze returns a LITERAL zero when
 there is no interest, so the two answers really are exactly zero or not, and a
 4-ULP tolerance would swallow a genuinely tiny bearing and send his eyes back to
 the road a frame early.

  next-gaze-yaw : RiderState, Real -> Real
  next-gaze-yaw (state) (want) =
    if (want ~0 0.0) == False then state.gaze-yaw + real-max (0.0 - gaze-swivel-rate) (real-min gaze-swivel-rate (want - state.gaze-yaw))
    else if real-abs state.gaze-yaw <= gaze-return-snap then 0.0
    else state.gaze-yaw - r-sign state.gaze-yaw * real-min gaze-return-rate (real-abs state.gaze-yaw * gaze-return-ease)

 While his head is turned the focus rises with the turn and HOLDS its peak; it
 eases out only once he is fully straightened.

  next-focus : RiderState, Real -> Real
  next-focus (state) (gaze-yaw) =
    if gaze-yaw ~0 0.0 then real-max 0.0 (state.focus - focus-decay)
    else real-max state.focus (real-min (real-abs gaze-yaw / gaze-release-angle) 1.0)

  next-rider-gaze : RiderState, List Segment -> RiderState
  next-rider-gaze (state) (segs) =
    let want = desired-gaze state (list-at segs state.segment)
    in let gy = next-gaze-yaw state want
    in RiderState { segment = state.segment, along = state.along, across = state.across, yaw = state.yaw, v = state.v, tilt = state.tilt, heading = state.heading, gaze-yaw = gy, focus = next-focus state gy }

Section: The Gawk

 Once he has NOTICED the pigs he is committed to the slow gawk for the REST of the
 segment: sticky and purely positional, and the crossing resets him next segment.
 Distinct from pig-ahead, which is the HEAD turn and ends once the pig is beside
 him; this is the MOTION hold, so it ignores the eyes-on-road gate and persists
 into the turn.

  gawk-engaged : RiderState, Segment -> Boolean
  gawk-engaged (state) (seg) =
    if seg.pigs-distract == False then False
    else state.along >= (gaze-pig seg.length (seg.width / 2.0)).along - gaze-look-dist

 THE GAWK BRAKE, which the rider folds into its min-of-brakes. Not engaged until
 he has noticed the pigs; from then on he eases toward the gawk speed by the same
 kinematic law the corner brake uses, timed to settle a little before the pig so
 he creeps PAST it at that speed, and then holds.

 `engaged` is the optional the zig returns: nothing at all until he notices.

  GazeBrake = record {
    engaged : Boolean,
    accel : Real
  }

  pig-gaze-brake : RiderState, Segment -> GazeBrake
  pig-gaze-brake (state) (seg) =
    if gawk-engaged state seg == False then GazeBrake { engaged = False, accel = 0.0 }
    else if state.v <= pig-gaze-speed then GazeBrake { engaged = True, accel = 0.0 }
    else pig-gaze-brake-easing state seg

  pig-gaze-brake-easing : RiderState, Segment -> GazeBrake
  pig-gaze-brake-easing (state) (seg) =
    let d = (gaze-pig seg.length (seg.width / 2.0)).along - state.along - pig-gaze-settle-dist
    in GazeBrake { engaged = True, accel = (pig-gaze-speed * pig-gaze-speed - state.v * state.v) / (2.0 * real-max d 1.0) }

Chapter: Rider
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Pose
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Cat
  cites Safari chapter Gaze
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The RIDER: his state and how he decides to move, one frame at a time.

 A port of wasm/rider.zig.

 THREE THINGS HAPPEN EACH FRAME. He picks a lean by BINARY SEARCH -- the held lean
 whose projected arc stays on the road and ends nearest an asymptotic centre
 target. He picks a forward acceleration, taking the most restrictive of several
 brakes. Then the physics integrates both, and if his real position has crossed
 into the next segment he is re-expressed in that segment's frame.

 THE BINARY SEARCH IS THE HAZARD NOTES WARNS ABOUT. Twelve iterations, each
 deciding on a float comparison after simulating up to two thousand physics steps.
 A difference of 1e-7 between f32 and f64 will eventually flip one of those
 comparisons, and from there the two rides are on different trajectories -- so a
 long trace comparison WILL go red and it will NOT mean the port is wrong. The
 check therefore compares ONE STEP FROM A SHARED STATE and never a trajectory.

 Deferred exactly as the zig defers them, and for the same reason: the cat's
 drawing and pose machinery is out of scope, but `cat-in-danger` is arithmetic and
 is ported, so the throttle hold is real here rather than a no-op.

 We say:

Section: Physics Constants

  yaw-per-tilt : Real
  yaw-per-tilt = 0.1

 The route never banks past this, and the focal pull-in normalises the lean by it.

  max-lean : Real
  max-lean = 20.0 * deg

  a-accel : Real
  a-accel = 0.010

  v-max : Real
  v-max = 2.5

  approach-intersection-dist : Real
  approach-intersection-dist = 60.0

  straighten-margin : Real
  straighten-margin = 0.05

  turn-danger-steps : Integer
  turn-danger-steps = 2000

  min-forward-progress : Real
  min-forward-progress = 25.0

  tilt-hold : Real
  tilt-hold = 2.0 * deg

  brake-decay : Real
  brake-decay = 20.0

  asymptote-tuning : Real
  asymptote-tuning = 0.30

  center-lane-epsilon : Real
  center-lane-epsilon = 0.04

  max-tilt-correction : Real
  max-tilt-correction = 1.0 * deg

  lean-search-iters : Integer
  lean-search-iters = 12

Section: One Physics Step

 A lean tip and an acceleration, integrated. He leans FIRST, then that tilt yaws
 the heading, then the new speed along the MID heading moves the position -- which
 is a midpoint rule, not an Euler step, and the halving is load-bearing.

 The absolute heading turns by the same delta as the frame-relative yaw.

  simulate-rider-step : RiderState, Real, Real -> RiderState
  simulate-rider-step (s) (tilt-step) (accel) =
    let tilt = s.tilt + tilt-step
    in let v = s.v + accel
    in let heading-change = yaw-per-tilt * tilt
    in let mid = s.yaw + heading-change / 2.0
    in RiderState { segment = s.segment, along = s.along + v * r-cos mid, across = s.across + v * r-sin mid, yaw = s.yaw + heading-change, v = v, tilt = tilt, heading = s.heading + heading-change, gaze-yaw = s.gaze-yaw, focus = s.focus }

Section: Projecting the Arc

 Project the held-lean, constant-speed arc forward and score how it plays out: off
 a shoulder is a bad lean, net-backward is a disaster, and clearing the forward
 progress is a good lean -- all pinned to the SAME forward, so survivors tie and
 the search then separates them on where they end up across the road.

 Side is which shoulder he left, if any.

  Side =
   | SideLeft
   | SideNone
   | SideRight

  PathSim = record {
    side : Side,
    forward : Real,
    crossed : Boolean,
    end-across : Real,
    frames : Real
  }

 The zig uses 1e9 as the frames answer for the arms that did not leave the road,
 and the shoulder brake only reads frames when side is not none, so the value is a
 placeholder rather than a number. It is kept because it is what the zig stores.

  no-frames : Real
  no-frames = 1000000000.0

  sim-loop : RiderState, Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Integer, RiderState -> PathSim
  sim-loop (start) (left-bound) (right-bound) (start-side) (start-along) (crossed) (i) (phys) =
    if i >= turn-danger-steps then PathSim { side = SideNone, forward = phys.along - start-along, crossed = crossed, end-across = phys.across, frames = no-frames }
    else sim-step start left-bound right-bound start-side start-along crossed i (simulate-rider-step phys 0.0 0.0)

  sim-step : RiderState, Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Integer, RiderState -> PathSim
  sim-step (start) (left-bound) (right-bound) (start-side) (start-along) (crossed0) (i) (phys) =
    let across = phys.across
    in let forward = phys.along - start-along
    in let crossed = if across * start-side < 0.0 then True else crossed0
    in if across < left-bound then PathSim { side = SideLeft, forward = real-min forward min-forward-progress, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = real-from-int i }
    else if across > right-bound then PathSim { side = SideRight, forward = real-min forward min-forward-progress, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = real-from-int i }
    else if forward < 0.0 then PathSim { side = SideNone, forward = forward, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = no-frames }
    else if forward >= min-forward-progress then PathSim { side = SideNone, forward = min-forward-progress, crossed = crossed, end-across = across, frames = no-frames }
    else sim-loop start left-bound right-bound start-side start-along crossed (i + 1) phys

 The bounds widen to wherever he already is, so a rider who starts outside the
 inset is not instantly judged to have left the road.

  simulate-rider-path : RiderState, Segment -> PathSim
  simulate-rider-path (state) (seg) =
    let inset-hw = seg.width / 2.0 - straighten-margin
    in let right-bound = real-max inset-hw state.across
    in let left-bound = real-min (0.0 - inset-hw) state.across
    in sim-loop state left-bound right-bound (r-sign state.across) state.along False 0 state

Section: The Lean Search

  want-more-right : PathSim, Real -> Boolean
  want-more-right (sim) (target) =
   when sim.side
    is SideLeft -> True
    is SideRight -> False
    is SideNone -> sim.end-across < target

 Binary-search the held lean whose arc stays on the road and ends closest to the
 asymptotic centre target. TWELVE ITERATIONS, each running a fresh path
 simulation, and each deciding on one float comparison -- which is exactly why the
 judge never compares a trajectory.

 The target is an asymptote rather than the centre line: he aims at a fraction of
 his current offset, so he converges on the middle without ever committing to a
 lean that would overshoot it. Dead centre gets a signed epsilon instead, because
 a target of exactly zero has no side to lean toward.

  lean-target : Real -> Real
  lean-target (across) =
    if real-abs across < center-lane-epsilon then (if across >= 0.0 then center-lane-epsilon else 0.0 - center-lane-epsilon)
    else across * asymptote-tuning

  with-tilt : RiderState, Real -> RiderState
  with-tilt (s) (t) =
    RiderState { segment = s.segment, along = s.along, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw, v = s.v, tilt = t, heading = s.heading, gaze-yaw = s.gaze-yaw, focus = s.focus }

  search-lean : RiderState, Segment, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  search-lean (state) (seg) (target) (lo) (hi) (i) =
    if i >= lean-search-iters then (lo + hi) / 2.0
    else search-step state seg target lo hi i ((lo + hi) / 2.0)

  search-step : RiderState, Segment, Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real -> Real
  search-step (state) (seg) (target) (lo) (hi) (i) (mid) =
    if want-more-right (simulate-rider-path (with-tilt state mid) seg) target
      then search-lean state seg target mid hi (i + 1)
      else search-lean state seg target lo mid (i + 1)

  best-tilt-correction : RiderState, Segment -> Real
  best-tilt-correction (state) (seg) =
    search-lean state seg (lean-target state.across) (state.tilt - max-tilt-correction) (state.tilt + max-tilt-correction) 0

Section: Throttle and Brakes

 The safe entry speed for a turn angle: the table from the TypeScript, and every
 angle on this route is tabulated. The comparison is on the ROUNDED degrees, which
 is what makes an exact match on a Real safe here.

  turn-speed : Real -> Real
  turn-speed (angle-rad) =
    let d = round-real (angle-rad * 180.0 / pi)
    in if d ~0 15.0 then 1.297
    else if d ~0 20.0 then 0.840
    else if d ~0 30.0 then 0.461
    else if d ~0 50.0 then 0.222
    else if d ~0 70.0 then 0.139
    else if d ~0 80.0 then 0.117
    else 0.222

 Accelerate, but only while near-upright, and then take the MOST RESTRICTIVE of
 the brakes: reach the corner's entry speed by the commit point, hold the throttle
 for a crossing cat, ease down for the pigs, and do not run off the shoulder.

 The order is the zig's and matters: the cat gate sits after the corner brake and
 before the shoulder brake.

  corner-brake : RiderState, Segment, Real, Real -> Real
  corner-brake (state) (seg) (v-end) (a) =
    let d = seg.commit-along - state.along
    in let corner-a = if d <= 0.000001 then 0.0 else (v-end * v-end - state.v * state.v) / (2.0 * d)
    in if corner-a < a then corner-a else a

 While the cat is in its danger window he stops CLOSING on it -- the acceleration
 goes to zero -- but he never brakes. Coasting in slows his approach, so he reaches
 the final segment later and there is more time for the sunset.

  cat-gate : RiderState, Segment, Real -> Real
  cat-gate (state) (seg) (a) =
    if seg.has-cat then (if a > 0.0 then (if cat-in-danger (seg.cat.along - state.along) state.v then 0.0 else a) else a) else a

  pig-gate : RiderState, Segment, Real -> Real
  pig-gate (state) (seg) (a) =
    let b = pig-gaze-brake state seg
    in if b.engaged then (if b.accel < a then b.accel else a) else a

 The decayed kinematic stop: the further ahead the shoulder is, the gentler the
 brake, and the decay makes a distant shoulder almost free.

  shoulder-brake : RiderState, Segment, Real -> Real
  shoulder-brake (state) (seg) (a) =
    let sim = simulate-rider-path state seg
    in if side-none sim then a else shoulder-brake-at state sim a

  side-none : PathSim -> Boolean
  side-none (sim) =
   when sim.side
    is SideNone -> True
    is SideLeft -> False
    is SideRight -> False

  shoulder-brake-at : RiderState, PathSim, Real -> Real
  shoulder-brake-at (state) (sim) (a) =
    let n = sim.frames
    in let sa = (0.0 - state.v) / (2.0 * real-max n 1.0) * exp-real ((0.0 - n) / brake-decay)
    in if sa < a then sa else a

 Do not crawl below the corner's entry speed approaching it -- UNLESS he is
 gawking at the pigs, where staying at the slow gawk speed and taking the corner
 slow beats snapping back up, because he never re-accelerates.

  clamp-v : RiderState, Segment, Real, Real, Boolean -> Real
  clamp-v (state) (seg) (v-end) (v0) (near) =
    let v1 = if v0 > v-max then v-max else v0
    in let v2 = if v1 < 0.0 then 0.0 else v1
    in if near then (if v2 < v-end then (if gawk-engaged state seg then v2 else v-end) else v2) else v2

  get-forward-accel-decel : RiderState, Segment -> Real
  get-forward-accel-decel (state) (seg) =
    let a0 = if real-abs state.tilt >= tilt-hold then 0.0 else a-accel
    in let v-end = if seg.terminates then 0.0 else turn-speed seg.exit-angle
    in let near = (seg.length - state.along) <= approach-intersection-dist
    in let a1 = if near then corner-brake state seg v-end a0 else a0
    in let a3 = pig-gate state seg (cat-gate state seg a1)
    in let a4 = shoulder-brake state seg a3
    in clamp-v state seg v-end (state.v + a4) near - state.v

Section: Deciding

  Decision = record {
    tilt-step : Real,
    accel : Real
  }

 The acceleration is chosen for the LEANED state, not the current one: he commits
 to the lean first, and the throttle then answers the bike he is about to be.

  decide : RiderState, Segment -> Decision
  decide (state) (seg) =
    let tilt-step = best-tilt-correction state seg - state.tilt
    in Decision { tilt-step = tilt-step, accel = get-forward-accel-decel (with-tilt state (state.tilt + tilt-step)) seg }

Section: Crossing a Seam

 Re-express the rider onto the next segment as he commits to the turn: the same
 physical point, read in the next frame. Speed and lean carry; the frame-relative
 yaw rotates by the turn while the ABSOLUTE heading does not, because the crossing
 changes the frame and not the bearing.

 His eyes go back on the road across the seam, but the FOCUS carries, so its slow
 re-widen keeps easing out instead of snapping open.

  rider-state-for-next-segment : RiderState, List Segment -> RiderState
  rider-state-for-next-segment (rs) (segs) =
    let seg = list-at segs rs.segment
    in let hw = seg.width / 2.0
    in let theta = seg.exit-angle
    in let sgn = if seg.exit-right then 1.0 else 0.0 - 1.0
    in let c = r-cos theta
    in let s = r-sin theta
    in let da = rs.along - (seg.length + hw * s)
    in let dx = rs.across - sgn * hw * (1.0 - c)
    in RiderState { segment = seg.exit-to, along = c * da + sgn * s * dx, across = (0.0 - sgn) * s * da + c * dx, yaw = rs.yaw - sgn * theta, v = rs.v, tilt = rs.tilt, heading = rs.heading, gaze-yaw = 0.0, focus = rs.focus }

Section: One Frame

 Decide the controls, run them through the physics, then resolve the road graph BY
 POSITION: commit to the next segment the moment his real point is inside it.

 At the terminus there is no segment to lead out, so he is clamped to the end and
 stopped. The snap fires once he has crept to the line OR slowed to a crawl inside
 the braking zone -- because the kinematic brake would otherwise Zeno-stall him a
 hair short and leave him stopped forever, never finished.

  finish-clamp : RiderState, Segment -> RiderState
  finish-clamp (moved) (seg) =
    let in-zone = (seg.length - moved.along) < approach-intersection-dist
    in if moved.along >= seg.length then stopped-at moved seg.length
       else if in-zone then (if moved.v < 0.1 then stopped-at moved seg.length else moved)
       else moved

  stopped-at : RiderState, Real -> RiderState
  stopped-at (s) (at) =
    RiderState { segment = s.segment, along = at, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw, v = 0.0, tilt = s.tilt, heading = s.heading, gaze-yaw = s.gaze-yaw, focus = s.focus }

  resolve-cross : RiderState, Segment, List Segment -> RiderState
  resolve-cross (moved) (seg) (segs) =
    let on-next = rider-state-for-next-segment moved segs
    in if real-abs on-next.across < (list-at segs seg.exit-to).width / 2.0 then on-next else moved

 The gaze is its own step and runs AFTER the bike has moved, because it reads the
 RESOLVED segment and position.

  get-next-rider-state : RiderState, List Segment -> RiderState
  get-next-rider-state (state) (segs) =
    let seg = list-at segs state.segment
    in let dec = decide state seg
    in let moved = simulate-rider-step state dec.tilt-step dec.accel
    in let resolved = if seg.terminates then finish-clamp moved seg else resolve-cross moved seg segs
    in next-rider-gaze resolved segs

  is-finished : RiderState, List Segment -> Boolean
  is-finished (s) (segs) =
    let seg = list-at segs s.segment
    in if seg.terminates then s.along >= seg.length else False

Chapter: Truck
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Rider
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The truck the rider is chasing: how far it has driven along the route, its speed,
 and whether it is slowing this frame.

 A partial port of wasm/truck.zig -- THE MOTION HALF ONLY, and the split is the
 zig's own. `State`, `initial`, `nextTurn` and `next` reach nothing but the world
 and a few of the rider's constants. The drawing half -- the trailer box, its
 tires, the headlight wedges -- reaches `render.Chain`, `render.Pose` and
 `render.at`, and it is `Safari chapter TruckBody`, which cites Render for exactly
 that. The split is what keeps World's cite of this chapter cheap: placing a truck
 costs five constants, and only the chapters that DRAW one pay for the mapping
 spine. Cat and CatDraw are split for the same reason.

 THREE RULES AND NOTHING ELSE: brake before a turn, accelerate when behind
 schedule, cruise when ahead. The schedule is a lerp -- it starts the truck five
 hundred metres ahead and closes that to a hundred by the course end, which is a
 photo finish rather than a catch.

 Every one of its margins is expressed against the rider's own constants rather
 than repeated: it accelerates ten percent harder, tops out ten percent faster,
 brakes over the same distance, and takes each corner at four fifths of the
 rider's safe speed. So a change to the rider's physics moves the truck with it.

 We say:

Section: The Chase Schedule

  start-ahead : Real
  start-ahead = 500.0

 The lead the schedule closes DOWN to by the course end -- not zero, because the
 point is a photo finish.

  finish-lead : Real
  finish-lead = 100.0

  truck-turn-caution : Real
  truck-turn-caution = 0.8

  truck-brake-distance : Real
  truck-brake-distance = approach-intersection-dist

  truck-chase-accel : Real
  truck-chase-accel = 1.1 * a-accel

  truck-max-v : Real
  truck-max-v = 1.1 * v-max

Section: State

  TruckState = record {
    pos : Real,
    v : Real,
    braking : Boolean
  }

 At the start: a long way down the road, idling at the rider's base speed, not
 braking.

  truck-initial : TruckState
  truck-initial = TruckState { pos = start-ahead, v = v-base, braking = False }

Section: The Next Turn

 How far to the next real TURN ahead of `pos`, and the rider's safe speed for it.
 Past the course end there is no turn to brake for and the truck cruises in.

 The zig answers infinity for "no turn", which Codex has no literal for. A sentinel
 would be worse than saying what is meant, so the absence is carried in the TYPE:
 `Maybe Turn` from `Foreword chapter Maybe`.

 IT USED TO BE A `found` BOOLEAN BESIDE THE PAYLOAD, which is a Maybe with the tag
 unhooked from the thing it guards -- nothing stops a caller reading `dist` without
 testing `found`, and nothing but prose says the two agree. `when` forces both arms
 and there is no unguarded field to read. The one caller reads better for it too.

  Turn = record {
    dist : Real,
    v-turn : Real
  }

  next-turn-loop : List Segment, Real, Integer, Real -> Maybe Turn
  next-turn-loop (segs) (pos) (i) (cum0) =
    if i >= list-length segs then None
    else next-turn-step segs pos i (cum0 + (list-at segs i).length)

  next-turn-step : List Segment, Real, Integer, Real -> Maybe Turn
  next-turn-step (segs) (pos) (i) (cum) =
    let s = list-at segs i
    in if pos < cum
      then (if s.terminates then None else Just (Turn { dist = cum - pos, v-turn = turn-speed s.exit-angle }))
      else next-turn-loop segs pos (i + 1) cum

  next-turn : List Segment, Real -> Maybe Turn
  next-turn (segs) (pos) = next-turn-loop segs pos 0 0.0

Section: One Frame

 Advance the truck one RIDER frame. `rider-dist` is how far the rider has now
 driven, and `l` is the course length.

 The kinematic brake arrives at the turn at the caution speed and is recomputed
 every frame, so it tracks rather than committing. The brake lights are lit only
 while it is ACTUALLY slowing -- the flag is a comparison against the previous
 speed, not against the intent.

 WHERE THE TRUCK "SHOULD" BE, given how far the rider has come: the lerp the
 whole chase hangs on, named rather than left inside `truck-next` because the
 browser's SCRUB needs it too. A scrubbed still has no state to step, so it places
 the truck on its schedule and accepts that the schedule is where the truck is
 AIMING rather than where three rules would have put it -- the same stand-in, and
 the same honesty about it, that the scrubbed rider gets.

  truck-schedule : Real, Real -> Real
  truck-schedule (rider-dist) (l) =
    rider-dist + finish-lead + (start-ahead - finish-lead) * (1.0 - rider-dist / l)

  truck-next : TruckState, Real, List Segment, Real -> TruckState
  truck-next (truck) (rider-dist) (segs) (l) =
    let scheduled = truck-schedule rider-dist l
    in when next-turn segs truck.pos
       is Just (turn) -> (if turn.dist <= truck-brake-distance then truck-braking truck turn else truck-free truck scheduled)
       is None -> truck-free truck scheduled

  truck-braking : TruckState, Turn -> TruckState
  truck-braking (truck) (turn) =
    let target = turn.v-turn * truck-turn-caution
    in let a = if turn.dist > 0.000001 then (target * target - truck.v * truck.v) / (2.0 * turn.dist) else 0.0
    in let v = real-max target (truck.v + a)
    in TruckState { pos = truck.pos + v, v = v, braking = v < truck.v }

 Behind schedule it accelerates, capped; ahead of schedule and not braking, it
 holds its speed.

  truck-free : TruckState, Real -> TruckState
  truck-free (truck) (scheduled) =
    let v = if truck.pos < scheduled then real-min truck-max-v (truck.v + truck-chase-accel) else truck.v
    in TruckState { pos = truck.pos + v, v = v, braking = False }

Chapter: TruckBody
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Safari chapter Render

 The trailer, the cab, the tires, the headlight beams and the brake lights: the
 truck as it is SEEN, where `Safari chapter Truck` is the truck as it MOVES.

 A port of wasm/truck.zig's `drawBody` and the three private drawers under it.
 SPLIT FROM Truck FOR THE SAME REASON CatDraw IS SPLIT FROM Cat, and the split is
 the zig's own seam read twice. Truck's motion half reaches nothing but the world
 and a few of the rider's constants, so World can cite it and every check that
 touches the world pays five constants for it. This half reaches `render.at`, the
 whole chain composition, the camera and the paint wire -- so it cites Render, and
 anything citing it pays for the mapping spine. Keeping them apart is what lets
 TruckCheck stay a nine-case motion oracle while this one grades a draw.

 IT IS THE LAST UNPORTED DRAWING CODE IN THE GAME. What unblocked it was Paint's
 tag 4: the beams and the brake glow are radial-gradient polygons, and until Paint
 modelled that tag there was no honest way to emit them.

 THE ZIG'S OWN COMMENTS SAY THE BEAMS AND THE GLOW ARE DEFERRED, AND THEY ARE
 STALE. drawBody's header and the brake-light block both claim the wedges and the
 halo still need "a gradient/alpha seam tag the polygon-only blitter doesn't have
 yet", and the file's top comment still calls the truck a placeholder blue dot.
 The CODE calls drawWedge and drawBrakeGlow and pushes tag 4 for both. The code is
 the oracle -- the port follows what runs, not what the comment says, and the
 check would have caught the difference either way, because a missing wedge is two
 missing commands and structure is graded exactly.

 EVERY VERTEX GOES THROUGH `at` AND THEN DROPS BY THE GROUND CURVE. That is `p3`
 in the zig, and it is the whole reason the truck reads as sitting on the road
 rather than floating above it: the body is authored in the segment-local frame of
 whichever chain index its centre landed on -- along the road, across from the
 left edge, metres up -- and the fake-horizon curvature is subtracted at the end,
 per vertex, so a long trailer bends with the road it is standing on.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

 Metres, and all of them mirror truck.ts's buildTruck. The trailer is narrower
 than the four-metre lane so it can round a corner without clipping the rails.

  truck-length : Real
  truck-length = 8.4

  truck-width : Real
  truck-width = 2.4

  truck-height : Real
  truck-height = 3.6

 The cab sticks this far out in front of the trailer, and its roof is this
 fraction of it -- the windshield rakes down over the rest.

  cab-length : Real
  cab-length = 3.5

  cab-roof-frac : Real
  cab-roof-frac = 0.3

  tire-radius : Real
  tire-radius = 0.5

 The trailer floor sits just above the tire tops; the cab floor sits at the tire
 centre line, which is the step the side silhouette walks up.

  trailer-bottom : Real
  trailer-bottom = 2.0 * tire-radius + 0.12

  cab-bottom : Real
  cab-bottom = tire-radius

  tire-pair-gap : Real
  tire-pair-gap = 1.2

  trailer-axle-inset : Real
  trailer-axle-inset = 1.6

  cab-axle-frac : Real
  cab-axle-frac = 0.55

  tire-sides : Integer
  tire-sides = 16

Section: Colours

 Three shades of the same dark blue, which is what makes the prism read as solid
 rather than as a flat silhouette: the sides are darkest, the rear and nose are
 the body colour, the roof is lightest.

  body-color : Integer
  body-color = 1846886

  roof-color : Integer
  roof-color = 3822248

  side-color : Integer
  side-color = 1384784

  brake-color : Integer
  brake-color = 16722456

  tire-color : Integer
  tire-color = 1381658

Section: The Headlight Beams

 Two WEDGES of light thrown forward from the cab-front corners, each a flat 3D
 side-silhouette -- a slit at the truck face fanning open down the road -- filled
 with a radial glow from the lamp. Its 3D angle alone decides how it reads: broad
 in profile, a sliver head-on. Shown only once the sun is behind the mountains.

  headlight-h : Real
  headlight-h = 1.0

  headlight-inset : Real
  headlight-inset = 0.3

  cone-near-half : Real
  cone-near-half = 0.25

  cone-far-center : Real
  cone-far-center = 0.7

  cone-far-half : Real
  cone-far-half = 0.9

  cone-length : Real
  cone-length = 20.4

 THE TWO-COLOUR WORDS ARE 0xAARRGGBB, NOT 0xRRGGBB, and that is what makes a beam
 light rather than a white wedge painted on the road. Warm white 255,248,214 at
 alpha 0.765 in the core, the same white at 0.27 at the edge. Paint's tag 4 is the
 only command besides the beacon that carries alpha at all.

  beam-core : Integer
  beam-core = 3288332502

  beam-edge : Integer
  beam-edge = 1174403286

 The brake-light halo: red 255,80,60 at alpha 0.9 falling to 255,42,24 at zero.
 The edge stop being FULLY TRANSPARENT is the point -- the halo has no rim.

  glow-core : Integer
  glow-core = 3875491900

  glow-edge : Integer
  glow-edge = 16722456

  glow-sides : Integer
  glow-sides = 16

Section: A Body Point

 A point authored in the body's frame -- along the segment, across from its left
 edge, metres up -- mapped into the rider's frame and then lowered onto the curved
 ground. The drop is a POSITIVE distance and the height is `h` minus it, which is
 the same sign convention Render's ground pass states.

  truck-p3 : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Real -> Vec3
  truck-p3 (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (along) (x) (h) =
    let r = at segs ch pose d along x
    in Vec3 { right = r.right, forward = r.forward, height = h - ground-drop r.right r.forward }

 The five abscissas and two edges every face is built from, computed once. `a0` is
 the trailer rear -- the face we chase -- and `a2` is the cab nose.

  TruckBox = record {
    a0 : Real,
    a1 : Real,
    a2 : Real,
    a-roof : Real,
    xl : Real,
    xr : Real
  }

  truck-box : Real, Real -> TruckBox
  truck-box (center-along) (hw) =
    let a0 = center-along - truck-length / 2.0
    in let a1 = center-along + truck-length / 2.0
    in TruckBox { a0 = a0, a1 = a1, a2 = a1 + cab-length, a-roof = a1 + cab-length * cab-roof-frac, xl = hw - truck-width / 2.0, xr = hw + truck-width / 2.0 }

Section: The Faces

 A face is a polygon in the rider frame plus the colour it fills with and the mean
 depth it sorts by. The zig recomputes that mean inside its sort's comparison;
 the port stores it, which is the same number and turns a repeated fold into one.

  TruckFace = record {
    color : Integer,
    fwd : Real,
    v : List Vec3
  }

  sum-fwd : List Vec3, Integer -> Real
  sum-fwd (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0.0
    else (list-at ps i).forward + sum-fwd ps (i + 1)

  truck-face : Integer, List Vec3 -> TruckFace
  truck-face (color) (ps) =
    TruckFace { color = color, fwd = sum-fwd ps 0 / real-from-int (list-length ps), v = ps }

 EACH SIDE IS ONE SILHOUETTE, not a stack of quads, and walking it is the clearest
 statement of the truck's shape in the whole file: up the rear, along the roof,
 down the windshield and the nose, back along the lower cab floor, up the step to
 the higher trailer floor.

  truck-side : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real -> TruckFace
  truck-side (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (x) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 x trailer-bottom
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 x truck-height
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof x truck-height
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 x (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p4 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 x cab-bottom
    in let p5 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a1 x cab-bottom
    in let p6 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a1 x trailer-bottom
    in truck-face side-color [p0, p1, p2, p3, p4, p5, p6]

  truck-rear : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-rear (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xl trailer-bottom
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xr trailer-bottom
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xr truck-height
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xl truck-height
    in truck-face body-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

  truck-roof : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-roof (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xl truck-height
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xr truck-height
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xr truck-height
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xl truck-height
    in truck-face roof-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

  truck-windshield : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-windshield (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xl truck-height
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xr truck-height
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xr (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xl (truck-height / 2.0)
    in truck-face body-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

  truck-nose : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-nose (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xl (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xr (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xr cab-bottom
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xl cab-bottom
    in truck-face body-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

Section: The Tires

 Five axles a side: a tandem pair inset from each trailer end and one under the
 cab. Each tire is a FLAT CIRCLE in its side plane -- no depth, no rim -- so it
 costs one polygon and still occludes correctly, because it joins the face sort.

  truck-axles : TruckBox -> List Real
  truck-axles (bx) =
    let rear = bx.a0 + trailer-axle-inset
    in let front = bx.a1 - trailer-axle-inset
    in let cab = bx.a1 + cab-length * cab-axle-frac
    in [rear - tire-pair-gap / 2.0, rear + tire-pair-gap / 2.0, front - tire-pair-gap / 2.0, front + tire-pair-gap / 2.0, cab]

 The circle is swept in the (along, height) plane at a fixed x, so it lies in the
 side of the body rather than facing the camera. Its centre is one tire radius up,
 which puts the bottom of every tire exactly on the road.

  tire-pt : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Integer -> Vec3
  tire-pt (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (ac) (x) (i) =
    let th = real-from-int i / real-from-int tire-sides * two-pi
    in truck-p3 segs ch pose d (ac + tire-radius * r-cos th) x (tire-radius + tire-radius * r-sin th)

  tire-pts : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Integer -> List Vec3
  tire-pts (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (ac) (x) (i) =
    if i >= tire-sides then []
    else [tire-pt segs ch pose d ac x i] & tire-pts segs ch pose d ac x (i + 1)

  side-tires : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, List Real, Real, Integer -> List TruckFace
  side-tires (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (axles) (x) (i) =
    if i >= list-length axles then []
    else [truck-face tire-color (tire-pts segs ch pose d (list-at axles i) x 0)] & side-tires segs ch pose d axles x (i + 1)

 SIXTEEN FACES, AND THE ORDER THEY ARE BUILT IN IS PART OF THE ANSWER. The sort
 below is stable, so two faces at the same mean depth stay in build order -- and
 the two sides are at the same depth whenever the truck is square-on to the eye,
 which is most of a straight. Build order is the zig's: both sides, then the rear,
 roof, windshield and nose, then the left tires and the right.

  truck-faces : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> List TruckFace
  truck-faces (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let axles = truck-axles bx
    in [truck-side segs ch pose d bx bx.xl, truck-side segs ch pose d bx bx.xr]
       & [truck-rear segs ch pose d bx, truck-roof segs ch pose d bx]
       & [truck-windshield segs ch pose d bx, truck-nose segs ch pose d bx]
       & side-tires segs ch pose d axles bx.xl 0
       & side-tires segs ch pose d axles bx.xr 0

Section: The Painter's Sort

 Farthest first, so the body occludes itself and its own far-side tires. The zig
 insertion-sorts sixteen faces in place and moves a key left only while the
 element before it is STRICTLY nearer, which is stable; this merges instead, for
 the reason Render's own sort gives at length -- an in-place insertion sort
 carried onto persistent lists gains a complexity class. Sixteen faces would
 survive it, but the answer is identical and there is no reason to keep two rules.

 It shares Render's `deeper-than` too, and for the same argument: two depths
 closer than half an f32 ulp are one number in the game and the port must not
 order them. The truck is where that is most likely to matter -- square-on to the
 eye, its two sides sit at exactly the same depth, and which one paints second
 decides nothing about the picture but everything about the command stream.

  face-rest : List TruckFace, Integer -> List TruckFace
  face-rest (ys) (j) =
    if j >= list-length ys then []
    else [list-at ys j] & face-rest ys (j + 1)

  merge-faces : List TruckFace, List TruckFace, Integer, Integer -> List TruckFace
  merge-faces (a) (b) (i) (j) =
    if i >= list-length a then face-rest b j
    else if j >= list-length b then face-rest a i
    else if deeper-than (list-at b j).fwd (list-at a i).fwd
      then [list-at b j] & merge-faces a b i (j + 1)
      else [list-at a i] & merge-faces a b (i + 1) j

  sort-faces : List TruckFace -> List TruckFace
  sort-faces (xs) =
    if list-length xs <= 1 then xs
    else merge-faces (sort-faces (list-take xs (list-length xs / 2))) (sort-faces (list-drop xs (list-length xs / 2))) 0 0

Section: Filling

 Near-clip, project, push -- the same pipeline a rail poly goes through, and it
 borrows GuardRail's `project-all` rather than restating it. push-poly's own
 under-three guard is what drops a polygon the clip reduced to a sliver.

  truck-fill : Integer, List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-fill (color) (ps) (cf) (view-w) =
    push-poly color (project-all (clip-near ps near) cf view-w 0)

  draw-faces : List TruckFace, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  draw-faces (fs) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length fs then []
    else (truck-fill (list-at fs i).color (list-at fs i).v cf view-w) & draw-faces fs cf view-w (i + 1)

Section: The Wedges And The Halo

 NEITHER GRADIENT SHAPE IS CLIPPED, AND BOTH ARE DROPPED WHOLE INSTEAD. The zig
 returns from drawWedge and from drawBrakeGlow the moment any corner is at or
 behind the near plane, rather than clipping it as a face is clipped. That is not
 an oversight to be improved on in the port: a radial gradient carries a centre
 and a radius in SCREEN space, and clipping the outline would leave the fill
 centred on a lamp that is no longer a corner of it. The truck is always at the
 far end of the chase, so the case is rare -- but it is the case where a clipped
 beam would be visibly wrong rather than merely absent.

  any-behind : List Vec3, Integer -> Boolean
  any-behind (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then False
    else if (list-at ps i).forward <= near then True
    else any-behind ps (i + 1)

  mid-vec : Vec3, Vec3 -> Vec3
  mid-vec (p) (q) =
    Vec3 { right = (p.right + q.right) / 2.0, forward = (p.forward + q.forward) / 2.0, height = (p.height + q.height) / 2.0 }

  pt-dist : ScreenPt, ScreenPt -> Real
  pt-dist (p) (c) =
    real-sqrt ((p.x - c.x) * (p.x - c.x) + (p.y - c.y) * (p.y - c.y))

 The gradient's radius is the FARTHEST corner, so the fill reaches every vertex of
 the shape it is filling and nothing is left flat at the tip.

  max-radius : List ScreenPt, ScreenPt, Integer -> Real
  max-radius (sp) (c) (i) =
    if i >= list-length sp then 0.0
    else real-max (pt-dist (list-at sp i) c) (max-radius sp c (i + 1))

 One wedge from one cab-front corner: near-bottom and near-top at the truck face
 (the slit), then far-top and far-bottom twenty metres down the road (fanned
 open). The lamp is the midpoint of the near edge and the glow is centred there.

  truck-wedge : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-wedge (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (src-x) (cf) (view-w) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 src-x (headlight-h - cone-near-half)
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 src-x (headlight-h + cone-near-half)
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d (bx.a2 + cone-length) src-x (cone-far-center + cone-far-half)
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d (bx.a2 + cone-length) src-x (cone-far-center - cone-far-half)
    in wedge-emit [p0, p1, p2, p3] cf view-w

  wedge-emit : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  wedge-emit (ps) (cf) (view-w) =
    if any-behind ps 0 then [] else wedge-cmd ps cf view-w

  wedge-cmd : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  wedge-cmd (ps) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sp = project-all ps cf view-w 0
    in let lamp = project (mid-vec (list-at ps 0) (list-at ps 1)) cf view-w
    in push-grad-poly beam-core beam-edge lamp.x lamp.y (max-radius sp lamp 0) sp

 Both beams, inset from each cab-front corner. They are pushed BEFORE the body so
 the opaque faces draw over them where they overlap -- mostly they spill onto the
 road ahead, which is what a headlight does.

  truck-wedges : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-wedges (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (cf) (view-w) =
    (truck-wedge segs ch pose d bx (bx.xl + headlight-inset) cf view-w)
    & (truck-wedge segs ch pose d bx (bx.xr - headlight-inset) cf view-w)

 The halo behind a brake-light panel: the panel's screen centre, a radius three
 times its own, and a sixteen-gon laid over it. It is a SCREEN-SPACE circle rather
 than a mapped one -- the light is a glow in the image, not a shape in the world.

  sum-x : List ScreenPt, Integer -> Real
  sum-x (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0.0
    else (list-at ps i).x + sum-x ps (i + 1)

  sum-y : List ScreenPt, Integer -> Real
  sum-y (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0.0
    else (list-at ps i).y + sum-y ps (i + 1)

  glow-pt : Real, Real, Real, Integer -> ScreenPt
  glow-pt (cx) (cy) (rad) (i) =
    let th = real-from-int i / real-from-int glow-sides * two-pi
    in ScreenPt { x = cx + rad * r-cos th, y = cy + rad * r-sin th }

  glow-circle : Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  glow-circle (cx) (cy) (rad) (i) =
    if i >= glow-sides then []
    else [glow-pt cx cy rad i] & glow-circle cx cy rad (i + 1)

  brake-glow : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  brake-glow (panel) (cf) (view-w) =
    if any-behind panel 0 then [] else glow-cmd panel cf view-w

  glow-cmd : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  glow-cmd (panel) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sp = project-all panel cf view-w 0
    in let cx = sum-x sp 0 / 4.0
    in let cy = sum-y sp 0 / 4.0
    in let rad = max-radius sp (ScreenPt { x = cx, y = cy }) 0 * 3.2
    in push-grad-poly glow-core glow-edge cx cy rad (glow-circle cx cy rad 0)

Section: The Brake Lights

 Two panels low on the trailer rear, drawn AFTER the body so they land on the face
 we chase rather than being sorted behind it. Each is a soft halo first and the
 bright core on top. They show only while the truck is ACTUALLY slowing, which is
 the flag Truck's motion half computes against the previous speed.

  brake-light : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  brake-light (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (a0) (x0) (x1) (cf) (view-w) =
    let bl = trailer-bottom + 0.20 * (truck-height - trailer-bottom)
    in let bh = trailer-bottom + 0.50 * (truck-height - trailer-bottom)
    in let q0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x0 bl
    in let q1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x1 bl
    in let q2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x1 bh
    in let q3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x0 bh
    in emit-brake [q0, q1, q2, q3] cf view-w

  emit-brake : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-brake (panel) (cf) (view-w) =
    (brake-glow panel cf view-w) & (truck-fill brake-color panel cf view-w)

  brake-lights : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  brake-lights (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (cf) (view-w) =
    (brake-light segs ch pose d bx.a0 (bx.xl + 0.10 * truck-width) (bx.xl + 0.36 * truck-width) cf view-w)
    & (brake-light segs ch pose d bx.a0 (bx.xr - 0.36 * truck-width) (bx.xr - 0.10 * truck-width) cf view-w)

Section: The Whole Truck

 Beams under, body over, lights on top. `d` is the chain index Render's truck-at
 found the centre on, `center-along` is how far along that segment it sits, and
 `hw` is that segment's half width -- the centre line the box is built around.

  truck-draw-body : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Boolean, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-draw-body (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (center-along) (hw) (braking) (headlights) (cf) (view-w) =
    let bx = truck-box center-along hw
    in let beams = if headlights then truck-wedges segs ch pose d bx cf view-w else []
    in let body = draw-faces (sort-faces (truck-faces segs ch pose d bx)) cf view-w 0
    in let lights = if braking then brake-lights segs ch pose d bx cf view-w else []
    in beams & body & lights

Chapter: Tree
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 A roadside conifer, with two independent detail axes the renderer sets per tree
 because they are noticed at different ranges: the TRUNK is a round-shaded cylinder
 up close and a flat bar beyond, and the CROWN is eight 3D cone tiers near and
 eight flat triangles far. A cone is a base circle of world points, each at its own
 depth, plus the apex, projected and filled to its convex-hull silhouette, so the
 near rim is genuinely nearer and foreshortens.

 The crown is SOLID either way -- its roundness is the SHAPE, not lighting -- which
 keeps it uniform across the tree line with no shaded-to-solid pop, since crowns
 are seen far out.

 A port of wasm/tree.zig.

 The hull is PORTED, not cited, though Math chapter Geometry has the same
 algorithm. Two reasons, and the second is the binding one. Geometry's diverges for
 fewer than three points -- it returns them sorted where the zig returns them as
 given. And chapters share ONE FLAT NAMESPACE inside a bundle: Geometry defines its
 own Vec3, which collides with Geom's, and the unit will not compile. Citing a
 foreword chapter is not free of a port's own type names.

 We say:

Section: Proportions

 The eight crown tiers as fractions of the foliage span: apex and base height, and
 base half-width. Narrow at the top, widest at the bottom.

  tier-top : List Real
  tier-top = [0.0, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.40, 0.50, 0.60, 0.70]

  tier-bot : List Real
  tier-bot = [0.30, 0.40, 0.50, 0.60, 0.70, 0.80, 0.90, 1.0]

  tier-wide : List Real
  tier-wide = [0.35, 0.44, 0.53, 0.63, 0.72, 0.81, 0.91, 1.0]

  trunk-color : Integer
  trunk-color = 5914146

  visible-trunk : Real
  visible-trunk = 0.44

  crown-h : Real
  crown-h = 0.648

  crown-w : Real
  crown-w = 0.288

 A cone base nearer than this bails to the flat tier.

  min-cone-forward : Real
  min-cone-forward = 0.4

  ring-n : Integer
  ring-n = 16

Section: Metrics

 The screen-space layout shared by both forms: ground point, pixel height, crown
 apex, foliage span, and crown base half-width.

  Metrics = record {
    bx : Real,
    by : Real,
    ht : Real,
    apex-y : Real,
    foliage : Real,
    w : Real
  }

  metrics : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Metrics
  metrics (right) (forward) (height) (cf) (view-w) =
    let base = project (Vec3 { right = right, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let top = project (Vec3 { right = right, forward = forward, height = height }) cf view-w
    in let ht = base.y - top.y
    in let foliage = ht * crown-h
    in let crown-bottom-y = base.y - ht * visible-trunk
    in Metrics { bx = base.x, by = base.y, ht = ht, apex-y = crown-bottom-y - foliage, foliage = foliage, w = ht * crown-w }

Section: The Trunk

  draw-trunk : Metrics, Boolean -> List DrawCmd
  draw-trunk (m) (round-trunk) =
    let trunk-w = if m.ht * 0.08 > 1.0 then m.ht * 0.08 else 1.0
    in let trunk-h = m.ht * visible-trunk + m.ht * 0.05
    in let tx = m.bx - trunk-w / 2.0
    in let pts = [ScreenPt { x = tx, y = m.by - trunk-h }, ScreenPt { x = tx + trunk-w, y = m.by - trunk-h }, ScreenPt { x = tx + trunk-w, y = m.by }, ScreenPt { x = tx, y = m.by }]
    in if round-trunk then push-round-poly trunk-color 1.0 pts else push-poly trunk-color pts

Section: The Flat Crown

 One flat crown tier: the far and cheap form, and the near form's bail-out for a
 cone too close to project.

  tier-triangle : Metrics, Integer, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  tier-triangle (m) (k) (color) =
    let tri = [ScreenPt { x = m.bx, y = m.apex-y + m.foliage * list-at tier-top k }, ScreenPt { x = m.bx + m.w * list-at tier-wide k, y = m.apex-y + m.foliage * list-at tier-bot k }, ScreenPt { x = m.bx - m.w * list-at tier-wide k, y = m.apex-y + m.foliage * list-at tier-bot k }]
    in push-poly color tri

Section: The Cone Crown

 Crown tier k as a cone: a base circle of world radius R on the trunk axis rising
 to the tier apex. Project the apex and a ring of base points, each at its own
 depth, and fill their convex hull -- exactly the cone's silhouette, a cone being
 convex.

 The ring angle is carried as a Real accumulator rather than derived from the
 index, because the zig plug has no emitter for real-from-int.

  cone-ring : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real -> List ScreenPt
  cone-ring (r0) (f0) (rad) (h-base) (cf) (view-w) (i) (a) =
    if i >= ring-n then []
    else let p = Vec3 { right = r0 + rad * r-cos a, forward = f0 + rad * r-sin a, height = h-base }
    in [project p cf view-w] & cone-ring r0 f0 rad h-base cf view-w (i + 1) (a + two-pi / 16.0)

 The tie test is ~0, BITWISE exact, not ~. Codex refuses == on a Real (CDX2085)
 and makes the caller say which equality it means; the zig writes a plain ==,
 which is the bitwise one. A 4-ULP ~ here would call distinct ring points equal
 and reorder the sort, changing the hull's point count.

  less-xy : ScreenPt, ScreenPt -> Boolean
  less-xy (a) (b) = if a.x < b.x then True else (if a.x ~0 b.x then a.y < b.y else False)

  hull-insert : ScreenPt, List ScreenPt, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  hull-insert (p) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then xs & [p]
    else if less-xy p (list-at xs i) then (list-take xs i) & [p] & (list-drop xs i)
    else hull-insert p xs (i + 1)

  sort-pts : List ScreenPt, Integer, List ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  sort-pts (src) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length src then acc
    else sort-pts src (i + 1) (hull-insert (list-at src i) acc 0)

 Trim while the last two hull points and the candidate turn the wrong way. The
 comparison is <= 0, so a collinear point is dropped rather than kept -- that is
 the zig's choice and it changes the point COUNT, which the judge compares exactly.

  hull-trim : List ScreenPt, ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  hull-trim (hull) (p) =
    let n = list-length hull
    in if n < 2 then hull
    else let a = list-at hull (n - 2)
    in let b = list-at hull (n - 1)
    in let cr = (b.x - a.x) * (p.y - a.y) - (b.y - a.y) * (p.x - a.x)
    in if cr <= 0.0 then hull-trim (list-take hull (n - 1)) p else hull

  lower-chain : List ScreenPt, Integer, List ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  lower-chain (ps) (i) (hull) =
    if i >= list-length ps then hull
    else let p = list-at ps i
    in lower-chain ps (i + 1) ((hull-trim hull p) & [p])

  upper-chain : List ScreenPt, Integer, List ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  upper-chain (ps) (i) (hull) =
    if i < 0 then hull
    else let p = list-at ps i
    in upper-chain ps (i - 1) ((hull-trim hull p) & [p])

 Andrew's monotone chain: sort, build the lower and upper chains, and concatenate
 dropping the last of each because it is the first of the other.

  convex-hull-pts : List ScreenPt -> List ScreenPt
  convex-hull-pts (ps) =
    let n = list-length ps
    in if n < 3 then ps
    else let sorted = sort-pts ps 0 []
    in let lo = lower-chain sorted 0 []
    in let up = upper-chain sorted (n - 1) []
    in (list-take lo (list-length lo - 1)) & (list-take up (list-length up - 1))

  tier-cone : Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real, Real, Metrics, Integer, Real -> List DrawCmd
  tier-cone (r0) (f0) (height) (color) (cf) (view-w) (m) (k) (shade) =
    let rad = crown-w * list-at tier-wide k * height
    in if f0 - rad < min-cone-forward then tier-triangle m k color
    else let h-base = (visible-trunk + crown-h * (1.0 - list-at tier-bot k)) * height
    in let h-apex = (visible-trunk + crown-h * (1.0 - list-at tier-top k)) * height
    in let apex = project (Vec3 { right = r0, forward = f0, height = h-apex }) cf view-w
    in let ring = (cone-ring r0 f0 rad h-base cf view-w 0 0.0) & [apex]
    in let hull = convex-hull-pts ring
    in if list-length hull < 3 then []
    else push-round-poly color shade hull

Section: The Tree

  tiers : Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real, Real, Metrics, Boolean, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  tiers (r0) (f0) (height) (color) (cf) (view-w) (m) (near-crown) (shade) (k) =
    if k >= 8 then []
    else let one = if near-crown then tier-cone r0 f0 height color cf view-w m k shade else tier-triangle m k color
    in one & tiers r0 f0 height color cf view-w m near-crown shade (k + 1)

 A tree less than a pixel tall is not drawn at all.

  tree-draw : Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real, Real, Boolean, Boolean, Real -> List DrawCmd
  tree-draw (right) (forward) (height) (color) (cf) (view-w) (round-trunk) (near-crown) (shade) =
    let m = metrics right forward height cf view-w
    in if m.ht < 1.0 then []
    else (draw-trunk m round-trunk) & tiers right forward height color cf view-w m near-crown shade 0

Chapter: Mountains
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Sky
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The far backdrop ranges, as screen-space silhouettes. Each range is a pure
 function from absolute bearing -- radians, 0 = north, + = clockwise -- to a height
 in pixels above the horizon; the frame samples it per column across the screen and
 fills the silhouette. Drawn FIRST, behind the road.

 A port of wasm/mountains.zig.

 The light DIMS the rock and the snow toward dusk, handed down as a `dusk`
 fraction from the sunset clock. The rolling LAND stays put, matching the grass.

 THE COLOUR HELPERS ARE SKY'S, NOT COPIES. snowColor is a lerp between two
 colours followed by a round and a pack, which is exactly `lerp3` and `pack` in
 Sky, and mountains.zig imports sky.zig for the sun as well. Reusing them keeps
 one rounding rule in the port instead of two that can drift.

 We say:

Section: Palette

  rock : Integer
  rock = 5991055

  rock-west : Integer
  rock-west = 3752799

  land : Integer
  land = 4886339

 Rock fades to this fraction of its day brightness by night -- it stays BELOW the
 snow, which is also dimming.

  rock-night-dim : Real
  rock-night-dim = 0.5

  snow-day : Rgb
  snow-day = Rgb { r = 238.0, g = 243.0, b = 248.0 }

  snow-night : Rgb
  snow-night = Rgb { r = 70.0, g = 84.0, b = 104.0 }

 Darken a 0xRRGGBB toward dusk. The multiplier keeps the rock always below the
 snow.

  chan : Integer, Integer -> Real
  chan (color) (sh) = real-from-int (bit-and (bit-shr color sh) 255)

  dimmed : Integer, Real -> Integer
  dimmed (color) (dusk) =
    let f = 1.0 - rock-night-dim * dusk
    in let r = real-to-int (round-real (chan color 16 * f))
    in let g = real-to-int (round-real (chan color 8 * f))
    in let b = real-to-int (round-real (chan color 0 * f))
    in bit-or (bit-or (bit-shl r 16) (bit-shl g 8)) b

  snow-color : Real -> Integer
  snow-color (dusk) = pack (lerp3 snow-day snow-night dusk)

Section: Shape

  west-range-bearing : Real
  west-range-bearing = 0.0 - 2.0416

 Only ridge taller than this gets snow, and this is how far the snowline dips
 under the summit.

  snow-threshold : Real
  snow-threshold = 124.0

  snow-dip : Real
  snow-dip = 10.0

 Column sampling step, in pixels.

  col-step : Real
  col-step = 2.0

 The camera rolls with the rider's lean, up to about twenty degrees, so the
 rotated viewport sees design-x beyond the screen at the corners -- about
 seventy-four pixels at full lean. Trace this far past each edge so a lean never
 reveals sky past the backdrop's end.

  roll-margin : Real
  roll-margin = 200.0

 One range: a smooth envelope, tallest at centre and tapering to open sky at its
 edges, times a fixed rugged ridge line.

  range-at : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Real
  range-at (bearing) (center) (half) (peak) (freq-a) (freq-b) =
    let b = wrap (bearing - center) 64
    in let t = b / half
    in if real-abs t >= 1.0 then 0.0 else range-body b t peak freq-a freq-b

  range-body : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Real
  range-body (b) (t) (peak) (freq-a) (freq-b) =
    let envelope = r-cos (t * pi / 2.0)
    in let ridge = 0.6 + 0.24 * r-cos (b * freq-a) + 0.16 * r-cos (b * freq-b + 1.0)
    in peak * envelope * ridge

  ground-base : Real -> Real
  ground-base (bearing) = 18.0 + 12.0 * r-sin (wrap bearing 64 * 0.9 + 1.9)

  north-range : Real -> Real
  north-range (bearing) = range-at bearing 0.0 0.95 150.0 8.0 21.0

  west-range : Real -> Real
  west-range (bearing) = range-at bearing west-range-bearing 0.72 120.0 11.0 27.0

Section: The Snowline

 The north range's tallest hump, for normalising the snowline dip.

 THE ZIG ACCUMULATES ITS SAMPLE POSITION IN f32 AND THIS ACCUMULATES IN Real, so
 the two do not sample the same set: 0.01 is not exact in binary, and the f32 loop
 drifts far enough to run 101 times where this runs 100. Measured, that moves the
 peak by 8.5e-6 out of 147.54 -- 5.8e-8 relative -- because a maximum over a
 densely sampled smooth function does not care which of two adjacent samples it
 lands on. It is recorded because it is the only loop in this chapter whose STEP
 is inexact; the column loops step by 2.0 from -200.0, which is exact in both
 widths, and both sides walk 681 and 481 columns exactly.

  snow-peak-loop : Real, Real -> Real
  snow-peak-loop (b) (vm) =
    if b > 0.5 then vm
    else snow-peak-loop (b + 0.01) (real-max vm (north-range b))

  snow-peak-height : Real
  snow-peak-height = snow-peak-loop (0.0 - 0.5) (0.0 - 1.0)

 The zig calls the normalised fraction `above`, and that name cannot be used
 here: `above` is a Codex KEYWORD, one of the proof-language set beside `claim`,
 `proof` and `qed`. The refusal is correct and the diagnostic is not -- it comes
 out as CDX1023 "Expected 'in' after let bindings", naming the let rather than the
 keyword, which is a bisect to find. See PORTING_NOTES B11.

  snowline-at : Real, Real -> Real
  snowline-at (bearing) (peak) =
    let num = north-range bearing - snow-threshold
    in let above-frac = real-max 0.0 (real-min 1.0 (num / (peak - snow-threshold)))
    in snow-threshold - snow-dip * above-frac

Section: Columns and Crests

 A screen column maps to a bearing through the LIVE focal: a smaller cam-focal --
 lean, or a focus pull-in -- widens the angular span across the screen, exactly as
 the projection does. The centre follows the live width.

  bearing-at : Real, Real, Real, Real -> Real
  bearing-at (x) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) =
    heading + r-atan ((x - view-w / 2.0) / cam-focal)

 The silhouette crest at a bearing: the tallest of the ranges and the land.

  horizon-crest-px : Real -> Real
  horizon-crest-px (bearing) =
    real-max (west-range bearing) (real-max (north-range bearing) (ground-base bearing))

 Has the sun dropped fully BEHIND the western range? True once the whole disc is
 below the crest at the sun's bearing -- the moment the truck's headlights come on.

  sun-behind-mountains : Real -> Boolean
  sun-behind-mountains (step) =
    sun-height-px step + sun-radius-px < horizon-crest-px sun-bearing

Section: Silhouettes

 Trace a range's crest left to right, then close down to the horizon: one filled
 polygon. `v-scale` squeezes the heights vertically by the same factor the pull-in
 squeezes them horizontally, so it reads as a real focal change.

 The range is passed as a FUNCTION, the way the zig passes it as a comptime fn.

  crest-pts : (Real -> Real), Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List ScreenPt
  crest-pts (f) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (x) =
    if x > view-w + roll-margin then []
    else [ScreenPt { x = x, y = camera-h / 2.0 - f (bearing-at x heading cam-focal view-w) * v-scale }] & crest-pts f heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (x + col-step)

  silhouette : (Real -> Real), Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  silhouette (f) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (color) =
    let top = crest-pts f heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (0.0 - roll-margin)
    in let close = [ScreenPt { x = view-w + roll-margin, y = camera-h / 2.0 }, ScreenPt { x = 0.0 - roll-margin, y = camera-h / 2.0 }]
    in push-poly color (top & close)

Section: The Snowcap

 The band between the snowline and the crest, over the contiguous central hump
 where the north range rises above its snowline. Top edge left to right, bottom
 edge right to left. The TypeScript clips the range to the cap; this builds the
 band directly, which is the same shape without a clip primitive.

  snow-columns : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List Real
  snow-columns (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (peak) (x) =
    if x > view-w then []
    else snow-columns-at heading cam-focal view-w peak x

  snow-columns-at : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List Real
  snow-columns-at (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (peak) (x) =
    let b = bearing-at x heading cam-focal view-w
    in let rest = snow-columns heading cam-focal view-w peak (x + col-step)
    in if north-range b > snowline-at b peak + 0.01 then [x] & rest else rest

  snow-top : List Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  snow-top (xs) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then []
    else [ScreenPt { x = list-at xs i, y = camera-h / 2.0 - north-range (bearing-at (list-at xs i) heading cam-focal view-w) * v-scale }] & snow-top xs heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (i + 1)

  snow-bottom : List Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  snow-bottom (xs) (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (peak) (i) =
    if i < 0 then []
    else [ScreenPt { x = list-at xs i, y = camera-h / 2.0 - snowline-at (bearing-at (list-at xs i) heading cam-focal view-w) peak * v-scale }] & snow-bottom xs heading cam-focal view-w v-scale peak (i - 1)

  draw-snow : Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  draw-snow (heading) (cam-focal) (view-w) (v-scale) (snow) =
    let peak = snow-peak-height
    in let xs = snow-columns heading cam-focal view-w peak 0.0
    in if list-length xs < 2 then []
    else push-poly snow ((snow-top xs heading cam-focal view-w v-scale 0) & snow-bottom xs heading cam-focal view-w v-scale peak (list-length xs - 1))

Section: The Frame

 Back to front: the westward range, the snowcapped northern range, and the rolling
 land. The rock and the snow dim toward dusk; the land does not.

  draw : Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw (heading) (dusk) (cam-focal) (view-w) =
    let v-scale = cam-focal / focal
    in let west = silhouette west-range heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (dimmed rock-west dusk)
    in let north = silhouette north-range heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (dimmed rock dusk)
    in let cap = draw-snow heading cam-focal view-w v-scale (snow-color dusk)
    in let ground = silhouette ground-base heading cam-focal view-w v-scale land
    in west & north & cap & ground

Chapter: Safari
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Sky
  cites Safari chapter Mountains
  cites Safari chapter Tree
  cites Safari chapter Tower
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Safari chapter Critter
  cites Safari chapter Cat
  cites Safari chapter CatDraw
  cites Safari chapter Gaze
  cites Safari chapter Pose
  cites Safari chapter Rider
  cites Safari chapter Render
  cites Safari chapter Truck
  cites Safari chapter TruckBody

 The whole game, folded: one ride state, one step of it, and one frame drawn from
 it. A port of wasm/safari.zig, and the last file in the game to be ported.

 safari.zig is the module the browser sees, and most of it is an ABI -- twenty
 exports handing the blitter numbers it cannot compute. THREE THINGS IN IT ARE NOT
 ABI, and those are what this chapter is:

   THE STATE FOLD. advance() is four calls in a fixed order and a restart at the
     finish line. The order is the whole content: the truck steps against the NEW
     rider distance, because its schedule anchors to where the rider is now.
   THE FRAME. renderFrame() computes the live focal and the view yaw, then calls
     render.frame -- whose outer sequence (backdrop, ground, everything that
     stands up in depth order) had lived in the browser poc because half its
     drawers were unported. They are all ported now, so it belongs here, above
     Render rather than inside it: the dispatch has to cite every drawer, and
     TruckBody cites Render, so a dispatch inside Render would be a cycle.
   THE CAMERA PULL-IN. The lens narrows as he leans and as he watches a crossing
     cat. `cam-focal`, `focal-for-lean` and `focal-for-gaze` have been ported and
     idle since Camera landed, because nothing above them existed to compute the
     two fractions they take. This is that thing.

 WHAT STAYS OUTSIDE, and it is a short list: the history ring, the exports, and
 the mutable statics that hold the current ride. Those are the shim's, and the
 shim is where they belong -- a Codex program is pure and a ring buffer is not.
 What the shim loses by this chapter existing is all the LOGIC it used to carry:
 the restart, the step order, the focal, the view-yaw fold. It keeps a value, an
 array and twenty exported readouts.

 We say:

Section: The Live Camera

 THE LENS PULLS IN -- narrows -- on two independent things, and the frame takes
 the deeper of them. Leaning into a corner narrows it toward 0.35x; watching a
 crossing cat or gawking at the roadside pigs narrows it too. `camera.camFocal`
 is a min over the two, so whichever is more distracted wins.

 THE WHOLE FRAME READS ONE FOCAL. The projection, the backdrop and the sun all
 take it, which is what keeps them one camera; the only place the STABLE base
 focal survives is the critter size cull, and Render's own prose argues that at
 length (culling animals on a leaned focal would pop them in and out of a corner).

  cat-attention : List Segment, RiderState -> Real
  cat-attention (w) (s) =
    let seg = list-at w s.segment
    in if seg.has-cat then cat-focus (seg.cat.along - s.along) s.v else 0.0

  ride-focal : List Segment, RiderState -> Real
  ride-focal (w) (s) =
    let lean-frac = real-min (real-abs s.tilt / max-lean) 1.0
    in let attention = real-max (cat-attention w s) (gaze-focus s.focus)
    in cam-focal lean-frac attention

 The extra camera yaw on top of his heading: the distracted gaze toward the pigs
 plus a subtle head-turn into a lean. It swings the WHOLE view, so it folds into
 the scene pose and the backdrop heading alike.

  head-yaw-frac : Real
  head-yaw-frac = 0.15

  view-yaw-for : RiderState -> Real
  view-yaw-for (s) = s.gaze-yaw + head-yaw-frac * s.tilt

  heading-for : RiderState -> Real
  heading-for (s) = s.heading + view-yaw-for s

  pose-for : List Segment, RiderState -> Pose
  pose-for (w) (s) =
    Pose { along = s.along, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw + view-yaw-for s, hw = (list-at w s.segment).width / 2.0 }

 THE BIKE'S LEAN AS THE CANVAS SHOULD ROLL IT, which is not quite the lean the
 simulation carries. The lean search leaves a residue around 1e-5 rad on a
 straight that never quite reaches zero; below a thousandth of a radian -- under
 half a pixel of bank at the screen edge -- the camera is upright for every
 practical purpose and the blitter's straight-line fast paths key on exactly
 zero. The simulation is untouched: this is a read, not a state.

  roll-deadband : Real
  roll-deadband = 0.001

 THE `let` IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT SHOULD NOT BE. Written as a bare
 `if real-abs s.tilt < roll-deadband then 0.0 else s.tilt`, this function is
 INLINED and never emitted, so the shim -- which is outside the program -- fails
 to link against it. Binding the field first makes it a real function. Measured
 over four variants: the same shape over a scalar parameter emits, and the
 arithmetic-bodied `view-yaw-for` beside it emits. PORTING_NOTES B12.

  rider-roll : RiderState -> Real
  rider-roll (s) =
    let t = s.tilt
    in if real-abs t < roll-deadband then 0.0 else t

Section: Drawing What Is Sorted

 The depth order Render produced, painted far to near. All six kinds draw.

  tree-fwd-of : List TreeItem, Integer -> Real
  tree-fwd-of (ts) (i) = (list-at ts i).fwd

 How many trees stand strictly nearer than this one. Only the closest four get a
 round shaded trunk, and since a roadside pair shares one depth, "fewer than four
 strictly closer" is the nearest two pairs.

  closer-count : List TreeItem, Real, Integer -> Integer
  closer-count (ts) (f) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ts then 0
    else if (list-at ts i).fwd < f then 1 + closer-count ts f (i + 1)
    else closer-count ts f (i + 1)

  crown-shade-of : Real -> Real
  crown-shade-of (fwd) =
    let raw = 1.0 - fwd / crown-shade-dist
    in if raw < 0.0 then 0.0 else (if raw > 1.0 then 1.0 else raw)

  draw-one-tree : List TreeItem, Integer, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-tree (ts) (i) (cf) =
    let t = list-at ts i
    in tree-draw t.right t.fwd t.height t.color cf camera-w (closer-count ts t.fwd 0 < 4) (t.fwd < detail-dist) (crown-shade-of t.fwd)

 A tower's four base corners are re-derived from its yaw at draw time and mapped
 through the frame that owns it, which is why the collector kept the recipe rather
 than the points.

  tower-base : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, TowerItem, Integer -> List RiderPt
  tower-base (w) (ch) (pose) (tw) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else [map-pt w ch pose tw.map (base-corner-ax k tw.a0 tw.x0 tw.yaw).a (base-corner-ax k tw.a0 tw.x0 tw.yaw).x]
         & tower-base w ch pose tw (k + 1)

  draw-one-tower : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, TowerItem, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-tower (w) (ch) (pose) (tw) (cf) (step) =
    draw-flat (tower-base w ch pose tw 0) (map-pt w ch pose tw.map tw.a0 tw.x0) cf camera-w (step + tw.off)

  draw-one-critter : Billboard, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-critter (b) (cf) = critter-draw b.right b.fwd b.height b.cp b.face-right cf camera-w

  draw-one-cat : CatItem, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-cat (k) (cf) = cat-draw k.right k.fwd k.height k.pose-idx k.lift cf camera-w

 THE TRUCK TAKES TWO THINGS THE COLLECTOR DOES NOT CARRY, and both come from
 outside the depth sort: whether it is braking, which is a field on the state the
 shim steps, and whether its headlights are on, which is the sunset clock -- the
 same `sun-behind-mountains` the backdrop uses, so the beams come on at the moment
 the sun leaves the crest rather than at a time of their own. Its half width is
 the width of the segment its CENTRE landed on, chain index `tk.d`, not the
 rider's own.

  draw-one-truck : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, TruckAt, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-truck (w) (ch) (pose) (tk) (braking) (cf) (step) =
    truck-draw-body w ch pose tk.d tk.along ((list-at w (list-at ch tk.d)).width / 2.0) braking (sun-behind-mountains step) cf camera-w

  draw-item : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Item, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-item (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (it) (braking) (cf) (step) =
   when it.kind
    is KTree -> draw-one-tree c.trees it.i cf
    is KTower -> draw-one-tower w ch pose (list-at c.towers it.i) cf step
    is KRail -> rail-draw-poly (list-at c.rails it.i) cf camera-w
    is KCow -> draw-one-critter (list-at c.cows it.i) cf
    is KCat -> draw-one-cat (list-at c.cats it.i) cf
    is KTruck -> draw-one-truck w ch pose c.truck braking cf step

  draw-order : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Boolean, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  draw-order (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (braking) (cf) (step) (i) =
    if i >= list-length c.order then []
    else (draw-item w ch pose c (list-at c.order i) braking cf step) & draw-order w ch pose c braking cf step (i + 1)

Section: The Ride

 ONE RECORD FOR THE WHOLE RIDE, because the three things advance together and a
 caller that could step one without the others would be a bug waiting. The clock
 is a plain frame count -- safari.zig's `step_clock`, which gains one per advance
 and gives one back on the way out -- and it drives the sunset, the sun and the
 tower beacons.

  Ride = record {
    rider : RiderState,
    truck : TruckState,
    clock : Real
  }

  ride-initial : Ride
  ride-initial = Ride { rider = initial-rider-state, truck = truck-initial, clock = 0.0 }

 ONE STEP, AND THE ORDER IN IT IS THE WHOLE CONTENT. The rider moves first and the
 truck steps against his NEW route distance, because the chase schedule anchors to
 where he is now; stepping it against the old distance would leave the lead one
 frame stale at exactly the moments it is tightest. The finish line restarts the
 whole ride rather than stopping, because this is a screensaver.

  ride-next : List Segment, Ride -> Ride
  ride-next (w) (r) =
    if is-finished r.rider w then ride-initial else ride-step w r

  ride-step : List Segment, Ride -> Ride
  ride-step (w) (r) =
    let nx = get-next-rider-state r.rider w
    in let dist = route-distance w nx.segment nx.along
    in Ride { rider = nx, truck = truck-next r.truck dist w (course-length w), clock = r.clock + 1.0 }

Section: The Frame

 render.zig's own sequence: the backdrop behind everything, then the floor, then
 everything that stands up, in one depth order. The live focal reaches all three.

  ride-frame : List Segment, Ride -> List DrawCmd
  ride-frame (w) (r) =
    let s = r.rider
    in let cf = ride-focal w s
    in let vy = view-yaw-for s
    in let pose = Pose { along = s.along, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw + vy, hw = (list-at w s.segment).width / 2.0 }
    in let ch = build-chain w s.segment
    in (draw (s.heading + vy) (sun-set-fraction r.clock) cf camera-w)
       & (frame-ground w s.segment pose cf camera-w)
       & (draw-order w ch pose (collect w s.segment pose cf s.along s.v r.truck.pos) r.truck.braking cf r.clock 0)

 The sun's placement for this frame, which the blitter paints itself behind the
 ranges. It reads the same heading the mountains do and the same live focal the
 rest of the frame does, so it cannot drift away from what is drawn over it.

  ride-sun : List Segment, Ride -> SunPos
  ride-sun (w) (r) =
    let cf = ride-focal w r.rider
    in sun-pos (heading-for r.rider) r.clock cf camera-w

Chapter: Drive
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Tree
  cites Safari chapter Tower
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Safari chapter Pond
  cites Safari chapter Mountains
  cites Safari chapter Sky
  cites Safari chapter SafariCritter
  cites Safari chapter Critter
  cites Safari chapter CatDraw
  cites Safari chapter Rider
  cites Safari chapter Render
  cites Safari chapter Truck
  cites Safari chapter Safari

 A browser frame of the REAL route, SCRUBBED -- every value on the page a function
 of one number `u` running 0..1.

 THIS CHAPTER USED TO BE THE DRIVEN PAGE AS WELL, and it is not any more. The
 driven half -- the state fold, the live camera, the frame's own sequence and the
 dispatch over the depth sort -- is `Safari chapter Safari`, a port of the game's
 own safari.zig, and this is a poc again: a still at a chosen point on the route,
 which is what it was written to be. What is left here is the scrub itself.

 This replaces poc/Scene, which placed its own scenery by hand because almost
 nothing was ported when it was written. Nothing here is placed by hand. The road,
 its corners, the conifers, the intersection towers, the guard rails and the pond
 all come out of `Safari chapter World` -- the same nineteen segments graded at
 3,822 values -- mapped by Render's chain and ordered by Render's depth sort.

 IT IS STILL A POC, AND HERE IS EXACTLY WHAT IS NOT REAL ABOUT IT.

 THE ANIMALS ARE HERE NOW. Their art is `Safari chapter EmojiStills`, generated
 from the game's own baked table by harness/bake_stills.py rather than
 hand-carried, and `Safari chapter Critter` places it. One still per species, 129
 polygons in all -- nothing per-frame is baked; the animals are static billboards
 that scale with distance, and every bit of motion in this scene is computed.

 THE BULL DRAWS FLAT. It is the only Fluent Color animal and 40 of its 43 polygons
 carry a 2-stop gradient, which paint.zig writes as wire tags 5 and 6 that Paint
 does not model yet, so the generated table flattens each to its first stop.

 THE CAT DRAWS TOO, from `Safari chapter CatStills` -- seven stills, not seven
 hundred, with `Safari chapter Cat` picking one per crossing step and computing the
 lateral travel and the hop continuously.

 AND THE TRUCK IS HERE, drawn by `Safari chapter TruckBody`.

 THE RIDER IS NOT SIMULATED ON THIS PAGE, and that is what makes it a scrub rather
 than a drive. `frame-at u` has to answer from u alone, so u walks the route by arc
 length at a constant speed, dead centre of the lane, looking straight down it: no
 lean, no gaze, no throttle, and a truck riding its schedule rather than its three
 rules. The driven page has all of those and is `Safari chapter Safari`.

 The backdrop heading is BLENDED ACROSS EACH CORNER rather than taken from the
 rider. A segment's north-heading is a step function -- it jumps by the whole turn
 at the joint -- and the mountains are drawn from it, so taken raw the entire
 horizon would snap sideways as the rider crosses. The real game does not have this
 problem because rider.zig turns him continuously. Blending over the last stretch
 of each segment is a stand-in for that, and it is the one number on this page that
 is invented rather than ported.

 We say:

Section: The Scrub

 u runs 0..1 and the shim advances it. Everything on the page is a function of it.

 The sunset clock for a SCRUBBED still, where there is no frame history to count.
 The driven page keeps a real frame counter (see The Real Rider below); this maps
 the scrub onto the same range by taking the route to be about 6,400 frames, which
 is what it measures at the rider's own speeds. Near enough for a still, and the
 stills are the only thing that still uses it.

  route-frames : Real
  route-frames = 6400.0

  scene-step-at : Real -> Real
  scene-step-at (u) = route-frames * u

 HOW FAR u MOVES IN ONE FRAME, AND IT IS THE ROUTE THAT DECIDES, not a constant
 picked to look right. rider.zig measures speed in METRES PER FRAME -- v-base 0.3,
 v-max 2.5 -- so a frame covers about two metres once he is up to speed and
 braking for corners. u is arc length over the whole course, so one frame is that
 distance divided by the course.

 The scrub had a flat 1/900, which over a 7.5 km course is 8.4 m a frame: more
 than three times the rider's top speed, and it looked it. Deriving it from
 v-max means the page cannot drift away from the game's own pace again, and a
 route change moves it automatically.

  drive-speed : Real
  drive-speed = v-max * 0.8

  u-per-step : Real
  u-per-step = drive-speed / (course-length build-world * 0.995)

Section: Where On The Route

 THE WORLD IS THREADED, NOT REFERENCED, and that is a performance fix rather than
 a style choice. A nullary Codex binding emits as a zig FUNCTION, not a memoised
 constant -- `segs = build-world` became `fn segs() { return build_world(); }` --
 so every mention rebuilt all nineteen segments with their trees, herds and cat
 placements. `pos-from` mentions it twice per loop iteration, so one frame rebuilt
 the world hundreds of times: 270 ms a frame, about four per second. Passing it
 down as a parameter, the way every graded chapter in the port already does,
 builds it once. See PORTING_NOTES B13.

 How far along the whole course, in metres. Stopping just short of the end keeps
 the walk below inside the last segment rather than falling off it.

  drive-dist-at : List Segment, Real -> Real
  drive-dist-at (w) (u) = course-length w * u * 0.995

  RoutePos = record {
    seg : Integer,
    along : Real
  }

  pos-from : List Segment, Real, Integer -> RoutePos
  pos-from (w) (dist) (i) =
    if i + 1 >= list-length w then RoutePos { seg = i, along = dist }
    else if dist < (list-at w i).length then RoutePos { seg = i, along = dist }
    else pos-from w (dist - (list-at w i).length) (i + 1)

  drive-pos-at : List Segment, Real -> RoutePos
  drive-pos-at (w) (u) = pos-from w (drive-dist-at w u) 0

 THE BLENDED HEADING, and the reason it exists is in this chapter's opening. A
 segment's north-heading jumps by the whole exit turn at the joint; blending into
 the next one over the last stretch keeps the mountains from snapping sideways.

  turn-blend-dist : Real
  turn-blend-dist = 120.0

  heading-of : List Segment, RoutePos -> Real
  heading-of (w) (p) =
    let sg = list-at w p.seg
    in if p.seg + 1 >= list-length w then sg.north-heading
      else blend-heading sg (list-at w (p.seg + 1)) p.along

  blend-heading : Segment, Segment, Real -> Real
  blend-heading (sg) (nx) (along) =
    let left = sg.length - along
    in if left >= turn-blend-dist then sg.north-heading
      else sg.north-heading + (nx.north-heading - sg.north-heading) * (turn-blend-dist - left) / turn-blend-dist

  heading-in : List Segment, Real -> Real
  heading-in (w) (u) = heading-of w (drive-pos-at w u)

  scene-heading-at : Real -> Real
  scene-heading-at (u) = heading-in build-world u

 The shim reads the sun straight off Sky, as the old scene did.

  scene-sun-at : Real -> SunPos
  scene-sun-at (u) = sun-pos (heading-in build-world u) (scene-step-at u) focal camera-w

Section: The Pose

 Dead centre of the lane, looking straight down it. across and yaw are the two
 numbers the rider would own, and both are zero here.

  pose-in : List Segment, Real -> Pose
  pose-in (w) (u) =
    let p = drive-pos-at w u
    in Pose { along = p.along, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = (list-at w p.seg).width / 2.0 }

Section: The Frame

 Backdrop, then the floor, then everything that stands up in depth order. That is
 render.zig's own sequence.

  frame-at : Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-at (u) = frame-in build-world u

 THE SCRUBBED TRUCK RIDES ITS SCHEDULE. There is no state to step here -- a still
 has to answer from u alone -- so it is placed where the chase says it should be
 for this rider distance, which is `truck-schedule` and not a number invented on
 this page. It never brakes, because braking is a comparison against a previous
 speed and a still has no previous. Same shape as the scrubbed rider above, and
 the driven page below does not use any of it.

  frame-in : List Segment, Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-in (w) (u) =
    let p = drive-pos-at w u
    in let pose = pose-in w u
    in let step = scene-step-at u
    in let ch = build-chain w p.seg
    in let tk = truck-schedule (drive-dist-at w u) (course-length w)
    in (draw (heading-in w u) (sun-set-fraction step) focal camera-w)
       & (frame-ground w p.seg pose focal camera-w)
       & (draw-order w ch pose (collect w p.seg pose focal p.along 0.0 tk) False focal step 0)

  frame : List DrawCmd
  frame = frame-at 0.0

Chapter: Spike
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Critter
  cites Safari chapter CatDraw
  cites Safari chapter Render
  cites Safari chapter Truck
  cites Safari chapter TruckBody
  cites Safari chapter Pose
  cites Safari chapter Rider
  cites Poc chapter Drive

 SPIKE ONLY. THROWAWAY. NOT PART OF THE PORT AND GRADED BY NOTHING.

 This chapter exists to put ONE still frame in front of a human, at a chosen point
 on the route, so a piece of scenery can be LOOKED AT instead of reasoned about.
 harness/spike_svg.py renders it to a flat SVG -- no wasm, no blitter, no
 animation -- because a picture you can open is a faster feedback loop than a page
 you have to drive to the right spot.

 Nothing here is verified. The graded port is `Safari chapter Render` and the
 checks in judge/; if this chapter and a check ever disagree, the CHECK is right.
 Delete this whole file the day it stops being useful and nothing else changes.

 IT ADDS NOTHING TO A NORMAL FRAME ANY MORE. Every kind that draws at all draws
 itself now -- the animals from EmojiStills, the cat from CatStills, both graded on
 the wire against the game's own functions -- so a still here IS what the browser
 draws, at a chosen point on the route and without having to drive to it. The only
 thing missing from both is the truck's body.

 We say:

Section: The Animals

 THE SPIKE STOPPED CHEATING FOR THESE. It used to emit a spike-only tag carrying a
 codepoint, and the SVG script read the game's baked table off disk to draw the
 animal -- because the port could not draw one at all. It can now: EmojiStills
 holds the art and Critter places it, graded at 5,916 coordinates against the real
 critter.draw. So the spike just calls the port, and the still shows exactly what
 the browser shows.

 The cat below still uses a tag, because its flipbook is a second table that is not
 generated yet.

  marker-of : Billboard, Real -> List DrawCmd
  marker-of (b) (cf) = critter-draw b.right b.fwd b.height b.cp b.face-right cf camera-w

  cat-marker : CatItem, Real -> List DrawCmd
  cat-marker (c) (cf) = cat-draw c.right c.fwd c.height c.pose-idx c.lift cf camera-w

Section: The Frame

 Drive's own dispatch with the two billboard cases filled in, so everything lands
 in the REAL depth order rather than being painted on top afterwards. That is the
 whole point: a pig behind a tree must be behind the tree.

 THE TRUCK IS DRAWN BY DRIVE'S OWN ARM, not a marker: `Safari chapter TruckBody`
 is real geometry rather than a baked still, so there is nothing here to stand in
 for. A spike still never brakes -- braking is a comparison against a previous
 speed and a still has no previous -- so its brake lights are always off, and its
 headlights follow the sunset clock exactly as the page's do.

  spike-item : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Item, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  spike-item (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (it) (cf) (step) =
   when it.kind
    is KCow -> marker-of (list-at c.cows it.i) cf
    is KCat -> cat-marker (list-at c.cats it.i) cf
    is KTree -> draw-one-tree c.trees it.i cf
    is KTower -> draw-one-tower w ch pose (list-at c.towers it.i) cf step
    is KRail -> rail-draw-poly (list-at c.rails it.i) cf camera-w
    is KTruck -> draw-one-truck w ch pose c.truck False cf step

  spike-order : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  spike-order (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (cf) (step) (i) =
    if i >= list-length c.order then []
    else (spike-item w ch pose c (list-at c.order i) cf step) & spike-order w ch pose c cf step (i + 1)

 THE LEAD IS A PARAMETER HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE, which is the one liberty this
 chapter takes with the chase. Every viewpoint below passes the truck's own
 SCHEDULE, so what a still shows is what the page would show at that spot -- a
 speck four hundred metres down the road, mostly. The truck viewpoint passes
 twenty-six metres instead, because a still exists to put one thing in front of a
 human at a size they can read, and the schedule never brings the truck that close
 until the finish.

  spike-frame-at : Real -> List DrawCmd
  spike-frame-at (u) = spike-frame-in build-world u (spike-scheduled build-world u)

  spike-scheduled : List Segment, Real -> Real
  spike-scheduled (w) (u) = truck-schedule (drive-dist-at w u) (course-length w)

  spike-frame-near : Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  spike-frame-near (u) (lead) = spike-frame-in build-world u (drive-dist-at build-world u + lead)

  spike-frame-in : List Segment, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  spike-frame-in (w) (u) (truck-pos) =
    let p = drive-pos-at w u
    in let pose = pose-in w u
    in let step = scene-step-at u
    in let ch = build-chain w p.seg
    in (draw (heading-in w u) (sun-set-fraction step) focal camera-w)
       & (frame-ground w p.seg pose focal camera-w)
       & (spike-order w ch pose (collect w p.seg pose focal p.along spike-v truck-pos) focal step 0)

Section: The Viewpoints

 Hand-picked spots on the route, each aimed at one thing worth seeing. The metres
 are route distance from the start; drive-dist-at divides by the same 0.995 the
 page uses, so these land where the page would if you drove to them.

 THE STILL USES A LEVEL CAMERA -- dead centre of the lane, no lean, no gaze --
 even though the page now runs the real rider and has all three. That is on
 purpose. A still is for reading the SCENERY, and a leaned camera rotates the
 whole frame and moves everything in it, which is exactly the confound you do not
 want when the question is where the herd is.

 They are also close in. The first set stood 90 to 470 m off, which put a 1.6 m
 zebra at about two pixels -- right at the cull floor, which is to say invisible.
 These stand 25 to 70 m short of what they are aimed at.

 THE BIG PIG HERD is a seven-by-seven block with jitter, placed 60 m before the
 end of a distraction segment. Segment 2 is the first of the two that carry one
 (49 pigs; every other segment has ten or none), it is 400 m long and starts at
 820 m, so the herd sits at about 1160 m and this stands 90 m short of it.

 A nominal speed, so the cat's crossing clock is live. A still with v = 0 freezes
 the cat at the start of its walk-in, which is the one pose it is never
 interesting in.

  spike-v : Real
  spike-v = 1.2

  spike-course : Real
  spike-course = course-length build-world * 0.995

  u-at-metres : Real -> Real
  u-at-metres (m) = m / spike-course

  u-pig-herd : Real
  u-pig-herd = u-at-metres 1130.0

 THE MID-TOWER. Segment 6 is the 1200 m leg, the only one long enough to stand a
 tower halfway down its own length rather than at an intersection. It starts at
 2120 m so the tower is at 2720, and this stands 140 m short.

  u-mid-tower : Real
  u-mid-tower = u-at-metres 2650.0

 THE DUCK POND at segment 12's exit corner, the one hand-drawn scene on the route:
 water, a mud bank, and three duck billboards floating on it. Segment 12 starts at
 5320 m and is 300 long, so the corner is at 5620 and this stands 70 m short.

  u-pond : Real
  u-pond = u-at-metres 5596.0

 A CORNER PAIR: segment 15's exit carries the zebras, an adult at the far outer
 corner and its baby beyond the turn. Segment 15 runs 6320 to 6620, so this stands
 20 m short of the corner. At the first attempt it stood 470 m off, which put a
 1.6 m zebra at about two pixels -- right on the cull floor, which is to say
 invisible, which is exactly what it looked like.

  u-zebras : Real
  u-zebras = u-at-metres 6600.0

 THE CAT, TWICE, because the interesting thing about it is a clock rather than a
 place. Segment 1 runs 500 to 820 and its cat waits at about 628 m -- world.zig
 rounds the desired 105 m up to just past a tree, so a tree stands between the
 rider and it. The first viewpoint catches it FROZEN in the lane, twenty-four
 frames of standing still; the second catches it AIRBORNE, mid-leap, which is the
 one place this port approximates and therefore the one worth looking at.

  u-cat-frozen : Real
  u-cat-frozen = u-at-metres 598.0

  u-cat-leap : Real
  u-cat-leap = u-at-metres 622.5

 THE TRUCK, TWICE, and the pair is the point: the same box in daylight and at
 dusk, so the headlight wedges can be looked at against a body whose faces are
 already known to be right. 2,400 m is mid-route on the long sixth leg, well clear
 of a corner, so the box stands square-on and its own painter sort is what decides
 which side is visible. 6,900 m is late enough that the sun is behind the crest and
 the beams are lit.

  u-truck-day : Real
  u-truck-day = u-at-metres 2400.0

  u-truck-dusk : Real
  u-truck-dusk = u-at-metres 6900.0

 Twenty-six metres: far enough that the whole trailer and cab fit the frame, close
 enough to see the tires are round.

  spike-truck-lead : Real
  spike-truck-lead = 26.0

Section: The Speed Profile

 SPIKE ONLY, and this one draws no scenery at all -- it is a GRAPH.

 It exists because two different things were making the page move wrong and they
 could not be told apart by watching it: whether the rider's own accel and decel
 are right, and whether a frame is being shown at the right rate. This isolates
 the first completely. It runs the real ported physics -- `get-next-rider-state`,
 graded at 199 values one step from a shared state -- for as many frames as it
 takes to cover the route, and reports the speed and lean at each. No animation is
 involved, so nothing here can be blamed on the clock.

 Read it as: v should sit near v-max on the long straights, dip into every corner
 by the amount turn-speed allows for that turn's angle, and climb back out at
 a-accel. A flat line means nothing is braking. A sawtooth with the dips in the
 wrong places means the corner brake is reading the wrong segment.

  ProfilePt = record {
    dist : Real,
    v : Real,
    tilt : Real,
    seg : Integer
  }

 One step of the real rider, recorded. The route distance is what the graph's x
 axis wants and the state carries segment and along, so route-distance converts.

  profile-pt : List Segment, RiderState -> ProfilePt
  profile-pt (w) (s) =
    ProfilePt { dist = route-distance w s.segment s.along, v = s.v, tilt = s.tilt, seg = s.segment }

  profile-from : List Segment, RiderState, Integer -> List ProfilePt
  profile-from (w) (s) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then []
    else if is-finished s w then [profile-pt w s]
    else [profile-pt w s] & profile-from w (get-next-rider-state s w) (n - 1)

 Four thousand frames is comfortably more than the route takes at v-max, and the
 walk stops early at the finish line anyway.

  profile-frames : Integer
  profile-frames = 4000

  speed-profile : List ProfilePt
  speed-profile = profile-from build-world initial-rider-state profile-frames

Chapter: SpikePrint
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Sky
  cites Poc chapter Spike

 SPIKE ONLY. THROWAWAY. How a still frame goes out as text, for
 harness/spike_svg.py to turn into a picture. Nothing grades this.

 A CHAPTER OF ITS OWN BECAUSE THERE ARE TWO ENTRIES NOW, and one of them cannot
 simply cite the other: a bundle is ONE FLAT NAMESPACE (PORTING_NOTES B5), so
 SpikeTruckMain citing SpikeMain for `all-cmds` collided on `opening` --
 "CDX3001 Duplicate definition: opening" -- which is the namespace rule doing
 exactly what it says. The printing moves here, both entries cite it, and neither
 restates it.

 COORDINATES GO OUT AS THEIR EXACT BIT PATTERNS. `show` on a Real is still refused
 by the zig plug -- it needs a __real_to_text it does not have -- so a Real reaches
 the page as an Integer either way. The question is only WHICH integer, and the
 answer stopped being a scale on 2026-08-30.

 THE SCALE WAS INHERITED FROM A DRAWING AND WAS SETTING THE RESOLUTION OF A PROOF.
 These lines were written for harness/spike_svg.py, which paints at 960 by 600,
 where a hundredth of a pixel is already invisible; a hundredth was therefore
 generous. Then `harness/metal.py --entry` began diffing the same text against a
 SECOND COMPILER, and there the last digit printed is the entire resolution of the
 comparison -- two emitters could have disagreed by four orders of magnitude and
 the run would have said AGREES. Millionths bought four orders. `real-to-bits`
 ends the argument instead of moving it: the pattern IS the value, so the diff is
 exact and there is no last digit to be generous with.

 WHAT THAT MAKES THE THIRD ARM ABLE TO SEE, which it could not before: FMA
 contraction, x87 excess precision, a reassociated sum -- every emitter fault that
 lives at 1e-16 and was invisible under any decimal scale (PORTING_NOTES D11 is
 where that limit was measured and admitted). It also makes the SVG exact rather
 than rounded, which costs nothing and was never the point.

 A pattern is a signed i64, so roughly half of them print with a minus sign and
 none of them are readable. That is the trade: this text stopped being for a human
 the day a second compiler started reading it.

 ONE LINE FOR THE WHOLE FRAME, commands separated by semicolons, rather than a
 print per command. A Codex `act` has no recursive loop shape, and a chapter is
 not the place to discover one for a throwaway: building the text purely and
 handing it to a single print is the boring option and it is fine here, because a
 frame is about a hundred kilobytes of digits.

   C <tag> <colour> <strength e-3> <n> <x bits> <y bits> ... [<colour2> <geom bits>...]

 We say:

Section: Printing

  scaled : Real -> Text
  scaled (v) = show (real-to-bits v)

  coords : List Real, Integer -> Text
  coords (ps) (i) = coords-range ps i (list-length ps)

 CONCATENATE BY HALVES, NOT ONE AT A TIME, and this is the difference between a
 spike that fits and one that does not. Text is immutable, so `a & rest` allocates
 the whole of `a & rest`; walking a list left to right therefore allocates the sum
 of its own suffixes, which is QUADRATIC in the length. A 2,705-command frame at
 four hundred characters a command is about 1.5 GB of intermediate text to produce
 one megabyte of answer. Splitting the range in half instead copies each byte once
 per LEVEL, so the total is n log n: the same frame costs about 24 MB.

 `text-concat-list` WOULD BE THE IDIOMATIC FORM and it existed the whole time --
 Types/Builtins.codex:213, emitted by this very plug, used by the wasm, C#, python
 and riscv emitters upstream. It copies each byte once where halving copies it once
 per level. The halving stays because it is measured, byte-identical and already
 here; the primitive is the right starting point for the next printer. C17.

 IT WAS BLAMED ON C6 FOR A WEEK. The bump heap never reclaiming is true and is why
 the peak is the total, but the peak was quadratic for a reason that had nothing to
 do with the allocator, and splitting entries was treating the symptom. Measured on
 2026-08-30: spike_cat_main peaked at 3.92 GB of the 4 GiB reserve before this
 change and 108 MB after it, for byte-identical output.

  coords-range : List Real, Integer, Integer -> Text
  coords-range (ps) (lo) (hi) =
    if hi <= lo then ""
    else if hi - lo == 1 then " " & scaled (list-at ps lo)
    else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
      in coords-range ps lo mid & coords-range ps mid hi

 THE GRADIENT TAGS TRAIL THEIR EXTRAS, so a tag 0 or 1 line is byte-identical to
 what this printed before them and the reader needs no new rule for the common
 case. A tag 4, 5 or 6 line carries its second colour and then its own geometry
 AFTER the polygon: the reader knows from the tag how many numbers to expect and
 the point count already told it where the polygon ended.

  extras : DrawCmd -> Text
  extras (c) =
    if c.tag >= 4 then " " & show c.color2 & coords c.geom 0 else ""

  cmd-line : DrawCmd -> Text
  cmd-line (c) =
    "C " & show c.tag & " " & show c.color & " " & show (real-to-int (c.strength * 1000.0)) & " " & show (list-length c.pts / 2) & coords c.pts 0 & extras c

  all-cmds : List DrawCmd, Integer -> Text
  all-cmds (cs) (i) = cmds-range cs i (list-length cs)

  cmds-range : List DrawCmd, Integer, Integer -> Text
  cmds-range (cs) (lo) (hi) =
    if hi <= lo then ""
    else if hi - lo == 1 then cmd-line (list-at cs lo) & " ; "
    else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
      in cmds-range cs lo mid & cmds-range cs mid hi

 One SCENE header per viewpoint, and it carries the sky and horizon colours too,
 because blitter.js paints those itself from the module's numbers and an SVG has
 to do the same or the frame floats on nothing.

  scene-line : Text, Real -> Text
  scene-line (name) (u) =
    "SCENE " & name & " " & show (sky-color (scene-step-at u)) & " " & show (horizon-color (scene-step-at u))

Chapter: SpikeTruckMain
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Sky
  cites Poc chapter Spike
  cites Poc chapter SpikePrint

 SPIKE ONLY. THROWAWAY. The two truck stills, and the entry that first made the
 split: `SpikeMain`'s header holds the rule and the measurements.

 THE ORIGINAL EVIDENCE WAS THE NATIVE CEILING. Six viewpoints in one process came
 to within a rounding error of the 4 GiB reserve, and adding a seventh exhausted
 it:

     cx heap: exhausted at 4294965457 + 7000 of 4294967296

 IT WAS READ AS A PAGE-PERFECT ILLUSTRATION OF C6 AND IT WAS HALF THAT. The bump
 heap does never reclaim, so the peak really was the total -- but the total was
 quadratic, because every printer concatenated left to right and so allocated the
 sum of its own suffixes. C17 has the measurement: the same two frames peaked at
 3.5 GB before the halving fix and 133 MB after it, byte for byte the same output.
 An allocator that never frees is very good at hiding an algorithmic mistake
 behind itself.

 We say:

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (scene-line "truck-day" u-truck-day)
    print-line-uni (all-cmds (spike-frame-near u-truck-day spike-truck-lead) 0)
    print-line-uni (scene-line "truck-dusk" u-truck-dusk)
    print-line-uni (all-cmds (spike-frame-near u-truck-dusk spike-truck-lead) 0)
  end
